                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Kissing is a common form of physical intimacy that involves the exchange of saliva and can lead to the transmission of certain infections.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. How long is considered \"too long\" for a kiss?\n2. What happens to the body during a prolonged kiss?\n3. Can kissing lead to the transmission of infections?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The length of time that is considered \"too long\" for a kiss can vary depending on the individuals involved and their personal preferences. However, a kiss that lasts for several minutes without a break may lead to physical discomfort or fatigue.\n2. During a prolonged kiss, the body may experience a range of sensations, including increased heart rate, heightened arousal, and shortness of breath. These sensations are a result of the release of hormones such as adrenaline and oxytocin.\n3. Yes, kissing can lead to the transmission of certain infections, including herpes and the common cold. It is important to practice good oral hygiene and avoid kissing if you or your partner have any visible sores or symptoms of illness.",
