                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStudent: Professor, if I were to spit a lot without noticing, I may have extra of what?\n\nProfessor: Well, let's think about this. When you spit, what is it that you're spitting out?\n\nStudent: saliva and mouth fluids, I guess.\n\nProfessor: Right. And what is saliva made of?\n\nStudent: Water, mucus, and enzymes.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. So if you're spitting out more saliva than usual, it's because you have more water, mucus, or enzymes in your mouth than usual.\n\nStudent: I see. So it's not because I have extra phlegm, disease, germs, or spittle.\n\nProfessor: Correct. Now, why might you have extra saliva in your mouth?\n\nStudent: I don't know.\n\nProfessor: Well, let's think about it. What are some things that can cause an increase in saliva production?\n\nStudent: Eating spicy food, being nervous, or having a dry mouth.\n\nProfessor: That's right. So if you've been eating a lot of spicy food, or if you're nervous about something, that could explain why you're spitting more than usual.\n\nStudent: I see. Thank you, professor.",
