                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A snowflake forms in a cloud.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Snowflakes are ice crystals that form when water vapor in the atmosphere freezes.\n- Clouds are made up of tiny water droplets or ice crystals, depending on the temperature and humidity.\n- When the temperature is below freezing, water vapor in the cloud can directly freeze into ice crystals, forming snowflakes.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) How do clouds form?\nAnswer: Clouds form when warm, moist air rises and cools, causing the water vapor in the air to condense into tiny water droplets or ice crystals.\n\n2) What conditions are necessary for snowflake formation?\nAnswer: Snowflakes form when the temperature is below freezing, and there is enough moisture in the air for water vapor to freeze into ice crystals.\n\n3) How do snowflakes grow and develop their unique shapes?\nAnswer: Snowflakes grow as they move through the cloud, collecting more water vapor and freezing it onto their existing ice crystal structure. The unique shape of each snowflake is determined by the temperature and humidity conditions it experiences as it grows.\n\nBased on the information and sub-questions, we can conclude that a snowflake forms in a cloud (option A).",
