                        "cot": "\n\nS: I don't understand this question. What does it mean by \"junk?\"\n\nP: Well, let's think about what each word in the question could mean. \"Sally was bored because she didn't like the junk that was on her...\" \n\nS: Okay.\n\nP: So, \"Sally\" is the subject of the sentence, which means that she is the one who was bored. \"Junk\" could mean a lot of things, but in this context, it probably means something that is not useful or wanted. \"Was on her\" means that the junk was somewhere near Sally. \n\nS: So the junk was near her and she didn't want it there.\n\nP: Exactly. Now, let's look at the answer choices.\n\nS: Okay.\n\nP: What does \"drawer\" mean?\n\nS: It's a place where you keep things.\n\nP: Right. And \"garage?\"\n\nS: It's a place where you keep your car.\n\nP: Exactly. Now, what about \"caddy?\"\n\nS: It's like a little cart.\n\nP: Right. And \"bed?\"\n\nS: It's a piece of furniture where you sleep.\n\nP: Exactly. And \"television?\"\n\nS: It's a device that you use to watch shows.\n\nP: Exactly. So, based on the definition of \"junk\" and the answer choices, which do you think is the correct answer?\n\nS: I think it's D) bed.\n\nP: Why?\n\nS: Well, the other choices don't make sense. If the junk was on her drawer, that would just mean she had a lot of stuff in her drawer. If the junk was in her garage, that would just mean her garage was messy. If the junk was in her caddy, that would just mean her caddy was full. But if the junk was on her bed, that would mean she didn't want it there.\n\nP: Exactly. Good thinking.",
