                    {
                        "id": "57afd231-a82a-4475-92d2-3b211680a2ca",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: Which type of melanoma is this 44-year-old African-American woman at the greatest risk for, given her background and examination results?\n\nStep 1: Consider the patient's background and risk factors.\n- She is a 44-year-old African-American woman.\n- Her uncle died of metastatic melanoma.\n- She has no history of serious illness and does not take any medication.\n- She works in a law firm and travels to the Caribbean regularly.\n\nStep 2: Evaluate the patient's examination results.\n- Examination of her skin shows no abnormal moles or warts.\n\nStep 3: Determine which type of melanoma is most likely for this patient based on her background and examination results.\n\nA) Desmoplastic melanoma is a rare type of melanoma that typically occurs in older individuals and is more common in sun-damaged skin. This patient is relatively young and has no abnormal skin findings, so this is less likely.\n\nB) Nodular melanoma is an aggressive type of melanoma that usually presents as a rapidly growing, dark-colored nodule. This patient has no abnormal skin findings, so this is less likely.\n\nC) Acral lentiginous melanoma is a type of melanoma that is more common in people with darker skin, such as African-Americans and Asians. It typically occurs on the palms, soles, and under the nails. This patient's background makes this type of melanoma more likely.\n\nD) Lentigo maligna melanoma is a type of melanoma that occurs in sun-damaged skin, typically in older individuals. This patient is relatively young and has no abnormal skin findings, so this is less likely.\n\nE) Superficial spreading melanoma is the most common type of melanoma, but it is more common in individuals with fair skin. This patient has darker skin, so this is less likely.\n\nAnswer: Based on the patient's background and examination results, she is at the greatest risk for Acral lentiginous melanoma (C).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "47bef9da-f0d1-4769-8f10-63ccf1c3cf2c",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Acral lentiginous melanoma.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/12 20:26:36",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
