                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: I'm not sure which type of melanoma this woman is at greatest risk for. Can you help me understand the different types and how to determine her risk?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by discussing the different types of melanoma. There are five main types: desmoplastic, nodular, acral lentiginous, lentigo maligna, and superficial spreading. Each type has its own characteristics and risk factors. \n\nStudent: Can you explain each type briefly?\n\nProfessor: Sure. \nA) Desmoplastic melanoma is a rare type that usually occurs on sun-exposed areas of the skin. It is characterized by its aggressive growth and tendency to recur.\nB) Nodular melanoma is an aggressive type that usually appears as a raised, firm, and dark-colored lesion. It can grow rapidly and is more common in older individuals.\nC) Acral lentiginous melanoma is a rare type that occurs on the palms, soles, and under the nails. It is more common in people with darker skin tones.\nD) Lentigo maligna melanoma is a slow-growing type that usually occurs on sun-exposed areas of the skin, such as the face. It is more common in older individuals with a history of sun exposure.\nE) Superficial spreading melanoma is the most common type and usually appears as a flat, irregularly shaped lesion with varying colors. It can occur anywhere on the body.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand the different types now. How do we determine her risk?\n\nProfessor: We need to consider her age, ethnicity, sun exposure, and family history. She is 44 years old, African-American, has no history of serious illness, and her uncle died of metastatic melanoma. She also works indoors and travels to the Caribbean regularly.\n\nStudent: So, considering her ethnicity and the fact that she has darker skin, would she be at a higher risk for acral lentiginous melanoma?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct! Acral lentiginous melanoma is more common in people with darker skin tones, like our patient. Additionally, her age and sun exposure do not put her at a higher risk for the other types of melanoma.\n\nStudent: So the correct answer is C) Acral lentiginous melanoma?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct! This woman is at the greatest risk for acral lentiginous melanoma due to her ethnicity and other factors.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that to me.",
