                        "cot": "\nThe correct answer is E) Superficial spreading.\n\nTo determine this, it is important to consider the patient's risk factors for melanoma. The patient is an African-American woman, which puts her at a higher risk for developing melanoma than other racial groups. Additionally, her uncle's death from metastatic melanoma increases her risk of developing the disease. The patient's occupation and travel to the Caribbean also increase her risk of developing melanoma due to increased exposure to ultraviolet radiation.\n\nBased on the patient's risk factors, the most likely type of melanoma she is at risk of developing is superficial spreading melanoma. This type of melanoma is the most common type of melanoma and is most often found in fair-skinned individuals who have had significant sun exposure. It is also more common in women than men.\n\nThe other types of melanoma listed in the question (desmoplastic, nodular, acral lentiginous, and lentigo maligna) are less common and are typically found in individuals with darker skin tones. Therefore, the patient is at greatest risk of developing superficial spreading melanoma.",
