                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what an intrauterine heteroechoic mass is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, an intrauterine heteroechoic mass is a mass that appears different from the surrounding tissue on an ultrasound image. In this case, it is located within the uterus.\n\nStudent: What does it mean when there are numerous anechoic spaces?\n\nProfessor: Anechoic spaces are areas on the ultrasound image that appear black because they do not reflect sound waves. In this case, the presence of numerous anechoic spaces within the mass suggests that it is a cystic structure.\n\nStudent: Why is there no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid?\n\nProfessor: The absence of a fetus and amniotic fluid suggests that this is not a normal pregnancy.\n\nStudent: What are the ovaries showing on the ultrasound?\n\nProfessor: The ovaries are enlarged and have multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content.\n\nStudent: What does it mean when the cysts have clear content?\n\nProfessor: Clear content within the cysts suggests that they are not filled with blood or other abnormal material.\n\nStudent: What is the most likely cause of the ovarian findings?\n\nProfessor: Based on the ultrasound findings, the most likely cause of the ovarian cysts is theca lutein cysts. These cysts are associated with high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which can occur in conditions such as molar pregnancies or multiple gestations.",
