                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the main findings on the transvaginal ultrasound.\n- Intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces\n- No identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid\n- Enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible causes of the intrauterine mass.\n- The presence of an intrauterine mass with anechoic spaces suggests a molar pregnancy or a hydatidiform mole.\n\nStep 3: Consider the possible causes of the ovarian findings.\n- Chocolate cysts are typically associated with endometriosis and are filled with old blood.\n- Corpus luteum cysts are a normal part of the menstrual cycle and usually resolve on their own.\n- Yolk sac tumors are rare ovarian tumors that can produce alpha-fetoprotein (AFP).\n- Luteoma of pregnancy is a rare benign tumor that can occur during pregnancy and produces hormones.\n- Theca lutein cysts are associated with high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) and are often seen in gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) or multiple gestations.\n\nStep 4: Determine the most likely cause of the ovarian findings.\n- The presence of multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content suggests theca lutein cysts.\n- The association with an intrauterine mass and absence of identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid suggests a molar pregnancy or GTD.\n\nAnswer: E) Theca lutein cysts",
