                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Analyze the ultrasound findings\n- Intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces\n- No identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid\n- Enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content\n\nStep 2: Match the findings with the possible causes\nA) Chocolate cysts - These are endometriomas, which are filled with dark, thick blood. They do not match the ultrasound findings of clear content.\nB) Corpus luteum cysts - These are normal during early pregnancy and usually resolve on their own. They do not typically cause enlarged ovaries with multiple cysts.\nC) Yolk sac tumor - This is a rare malignant germ cell tumor, which may cause enlarged ovaries but does not match the ultrasound findings of multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content.\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy - These are benign ovarian masses that can occur during pregnancy. They may cause enlarged ovaries but do not typically present with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content.\nE) Theca lutein cysts - These are benign ovarian cysts that can occur in response to high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). They can cause enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content, which matches the ultrasound findings.\n\nStep 3: Determine the most likely cause\nBased on the ultrasound findings and the characteristics of each possible cause, the most likely cause of the ovarian findings is:\n\nE) Theca leutein cysts",
