                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Analyze the information given in the question stem.\n- Intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces\n- No identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid\n- Enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible causes of the ovarian findings.\nA) Chocolate cysts - These are endometriomas, which are filled with old blood and have a thick, dark appearance on ultrasound. This does not match the description of the cysts in the question.\nB) Corpus luteum cysts - These are normal, functional cysts that can occur during the menstrual cycle. They are not typically associated with an intrauterine mass or enlarged ovaries.\nC) Yolk sac tumor - This is a malignant germ cell tumor that can cause enlarged ovaries, but it is not typically associated with an intrauterine mass or the described cysts.\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy - This is a benign ovarian mass that can occur during pregnancy, but it is not typically associated with an intrauterine mass or the described cysts.\nE) Theca leutein cysts - These are benign, functional cysts that can occur in response to high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). They can be associated with an intrauterine mass and enlarged ovaries.\n\nStep 3: Determine the most likely cause of the ovarian findings based on the information given in the question stem and the characteristics of each possible cause.\n\nThe most likely cause of the ovarian findings is E) Theca leutein cysts, as they are associated with an intrauterine mass and enlarged ovaries, and match the description of the cysts in the question.",
                        "answers": [
