                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: What is the most likely cause of the ovarian findings in a transvaginal ultrasound that shows an intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid, and both ovaries are enlarged with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content?\n\nStep 1: Analyze the ultrasound findings\n- Intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces\n- No identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid\n- Enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content\n\nStep 2: Evaluate each option in relation to the ultrasound findings\nA) Chocolate cysts: These are endometriomas, which are filled with old blood and have a thick wall. They do not match the description of the cysts in the ultrasound.\nB) Corpus luteum cysts: These cysts are formed after ovulation and can be filled with fluid or blood. They are usually unilateral and do not cause bilateral ovarian enlargement.\nC) Yolk sac tumor: This is a malignant germ cell tumor that can cause ovarian enlargement, but it does not typically present with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts.\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy: These are benign ovarian masses that can occur during pregnancy, but they do not present with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts.\nE) Theca lutein cysts: These cysts are associated with high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) and can cause bilateral ovarian enlargement. They are thin-walled and filled with clear fluid, which matches the description of the cysts in the ultrasound.\n\nStep 3: Determine the most likely cause\nBased on the ultrasound findings and the characteristics of each option, the most likely cause of the ovarian findings is E) Theca lutein cysts.",
                        "answers": [
