                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStep 1: Understand the question.\nThe question is asking which of the given options is the most likely cause of the ovarian findings described in the ultrasound.\n\nStep 2: Review the given options.\nA) Chocolate cysts\nB) Corpus luteum cysts\nC) Yolk sac tumor\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy\nE) Theca leutein cysts\n\nStep 3: Review the ultrasound findings.\nThe ultrasound findings include an intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid. Both the ovaries are enlarged and have multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content.\n\nStep 4: Analyze the given options in relation to the ultrasound findings.\nA) Chocolate cysts: Chocolate cysts are typically associated with endometriosis, which is not indicated by the ultrasound findings.\nB) Corpus luteum cysts: Corpus luteum cysts are typically associated with ovulation, which is not indicated by the ultrasound findings.\nC) Yolk sac tumor: Yolk sac tumors are typically associated with malignant tumors, which is not indicated by the ultrasound findings.\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy: Luteoma of pregnancy is typically associated with an enlarged corpus luteum, which is not indicated by the ultrasound findings.\nE) Theca leutein cysts: Theca leutein cysts are typically associated with ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome, which is indicated by the ultrasound findings of enlarged ovaries and multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content.\n\nStep 5: Answer the question.\nThe most likely cause of the ovarian findings is Theca leutein cysts (Option E).",
