                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nFirst, let's rephrase the question: What is the most likely cause of the ovarian findings in a transvaginal ultrasound showing an intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid, and both ovaries enlarged with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content?\n\nSecond, let's work this out step by step: \nThe transvaginal ultrasound shows an intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid. This suggests that the patient is not pregnant. The ovaries are enlarged and have multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content. This suggests that the cysts are filled with fluid. \n\nThird, let's make sure we have the right answer: \nBased on the information provided, the most likely cause of the ovarian findings is E) Theca leutein cysts. Theca leutein cysts are fluid-filled cysts that are commonly seen in non-pregnant women. They are thin-walled and septated, which matches the description of the cysts seen in the ultrasound.",
