                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The description of the ultrasound findings suggests a molar pregnancy (intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid). Molar pregnancies are associated with elevated levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which can cause ovarian changes.\n\nNow let's analyze the options:\n\nA) Chocolate cysts: These are endometriomas, which are not related to molar pregnancies or elevated hCG levels.\n\nB) Corpus luteum cysts: These can occur during normal pregnancies, but the description of multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content does not fit the typical appearance of corpus luteum cysts.\n\nC) Yolk sac tumor: This is a malignant germ cell tumor, not related to molar pregnancies or elevated hCG levels.\n\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy: These are benign ovarian masses that can occur during pregnancy, but they are solid and not characterized by multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content.\n\nE) Theca lutein cysts: These are ovarian cysts that can occur in response to elevated hCG levels, such as in molar pregnancies. They are characterized by multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content, which matches the description in the question.\n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of the ovarian findings is E) Theca lutein cysts.",
