                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what pale conjunctivae means?\n\nProfessor: Pale conjunctivae means that the lining of the eyelids and the white part of the eye appear pale or less red than normal. This can be a sign of anemia.\n\nStudent: What is anemia?\n\nProfessor: Anemia is a condition in which there are not enough red blood cells or hemoglobin in the blood. This can cause symptoms such as fatigue, weakness, and pale skin.\n\nStudent: What is basophilic stippling?\n\nProfessor: Basophilic stippling is a term used to describe small, dark blue or purple granules that are seen in red blood cells under a microscope. This can be a sign of certain types of anemia, such as lead poisoning or thalassemia.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what each of the answer choices means?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Option A refers to the conversion of iron from one form to another, which is important for the production of hemoglobin. Option B refers to a step in the synthesis of heme, which is a component of hemoglobin. Option C also refers to a step in the synthesis of heme. Option D refers to a process involved in the metabolism of amino acids. Option E refers to a step in the synthesis of heme.\n\nStudent: Which of these processes is most likely impaired in this patient?\n\nProfessor: Based on the symptoms and laboratory findings, the most likely answer is option C, which is the conversion of aminolevulinic acid to porphobilinogen. This is a key step in the synthesis of heme, and a deficiency in this enzyme can lead to a type of anemia called sideroblastic anemia.",
                        "answers": [
