                    {
                        "id": "3b7d9152-677a-47d3-a5f7-9534a3041ff9",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Thyroid nodules are common and usually benign, but a small percentage can be malignant. The most common type of thyroid cancer is papillary thyroid cancer, which often spreads to nearby lymph nodes before spreading to distant sites. Radioiodine therapy is a common treatment for thyroid cancer, as thyroid cells absorb iodine. Metastasis to the trachea, hilar lymph nodes, cervical lymph nodes, inferior thyroid arteries, and thyrohyoid muscle are all possible but vary in likelihood.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely type of thyroid cancer in this patient?\nAnswer: The type of thyroid cancer is not specified in the information given.\n2. Has the cancer spread beyond the thyroid gland?\nAnswer: Yes, the cytological examination of the fine needle biopsy reported a likely suspicion of neoplasia, indicating that cancer cells were present in the nodule. The CT scan is being performed to check for any lesions in the bones and/or lungs, common metastatic sites in this condition.\n3. What is the purpose of near-total thyroidectomy?\nAnswer: Near-total thyroidectomy is a common treatment for thyroid cancer, as it removes most of the thyroid gland and any remaining cancer cells. This is often followed by radioiodine therapy to target any remaining thyroid cells.\n4. Which sites are common metastatic sites for thyroid cancer?\nAnswer: The most common site for metastasis in thyroid cancer is nearby lymph nodes, particularly in the neck. Metastasis to distant sites such as the bones and lungs is also possible but less common.\n\nAnswer: Based on the information given, the most likely initial metastatic site in this patient is C) Cervical lymph nodes. The patient presented with a fixed, immobile, non-tender thyroid nodule, and the cytological examination reported a likely suspicion of neoplasia. Papillary thyroid cancer, the most common type of thyroid cancer, often spreads to nearby lymph nodes before spreading to distant sites. The CT scan is being performed to check for any lesions in the bones and/or lungs, which are less common but still possible sites of metastasis.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8374a5b7-9926-49c5-bbf6-ec5e5b8f37a6",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
