                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: In a 48-year-old female patient with a suspected thyroid neoplasm, which of the following is the most likely initial metastatic site?\nA) Trachea\nB) Hilar lymph nodes\nC) Cervical lymph nodes\nD) Inferior thyroid arteries\nE) Thyrohyoid muscle\n\nStep 1: Understanding the patient's condition\nThe patient has a thyroid nodule that is fixed, immobile, and non-tender. Ultrasound showed a hypoechoic nodule with a size of 2 cm. Histological examination of a fine needle biopsy reported a likely suspicion of neoplasia. A CT scan is performed to check for any lesions in the bones and/or lungs, which are common metastatic sites in this condition.\n\nStep 2: Identifying the most likely initial metastatic site\nIn cases of thyroid neoplasms, the most common initial metastatic site is the regional lymph nodes. Among the given options, the cervical lymph nodes (option C) are the regional lymph nodes for the thyroid gland. Therefore, the most likely initial metastatic site in this patient would be the cervical lymph nodes.\n\nStep 3: Verifying the answer\nThe other options provided are not the most likely initial metastatic sites for a thyroid neoplasm. The trachea (option A), inferior thyroid arteries (option D), and thyrohyoid muscle (option E) are all anatomically close to the thyroid gland but are not the primary sites for metastasis. Hilar lymph nodes (option B) are associated with lung metastasis, which is not the initial metastatic site in this case.\n\nAnswer: The most likely initial metastatic site in this patient with a suspected thyroid neoplasm is C) Cervical lymph nodes.",
