                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the most likely initial metastatic site for a thyroid tumor in this patient. We know that the patient has a thyroid nodule that is fixed, immobile, and non-tender, and the cytological examination suggests neoplasia. We also know that the CT scan is being performed to check for lesions in the bones and lungs, which are common metastatic sites for this condition. \n\nSub-questions:\n1) What are the common metastatic sites for thyroid cancer?\n2) Which of the given options is most likely to be the initial metastatic site?\n\n1) Common metastatic sites for thyroid cancer include regional lymph nodes (such as cervical lymph nodes), lungs, and bones.\n\n2) Considering the given options:\nA) Trachea - Thyroid tumors can invade the trachea, but it is not a common initial metastatic site.\nB) Hilar lymph nodes - These are located near the lungs and are not the most likely initial metastatic site for a thyroid tumor.\nC) Cervical lymph nodes - These are regional lymph nodes and are a common initial metastatic site for thyroid cancer.\nD) Inferior thyroid arteries - These are blood vessels, not a common initial metastatic site for thyroid cancer.\nE) Thyrohyoid muscle - This is a muscle near the thyroid gland, but it is not a common initial metastatic site for thyroid cancer.\n\nAnswer: Based on the information provided and the common metastatic sites for thyroid cancer, the most likely initial metastatic site in this patient is C) Cervical lymph nodes.",
