                    },
                    {
                        "id": "82209c5e-a05b-42c3-a0d8-9205b0e41055",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding this case. Can you help me figure out the next best step in the management of this patient?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by discussing the patient's symptoms. What do you think is the most likely diagnosis based on his presentation?\n\nStudent: Well, he has fatigue, weakness, abdominal pain, constipation, and weight loss. He also has a bronze discoloration of his skin. I think he might have Addison's disease.\n\nProfessor: Good thinking! Addison's disease, or primary adrenal insufficiency, is a possibility given his symptoms. Now, let's go through the answer choices and see which one would be the most appropriate next step in managing this patient.\n\nStudent: Okay. Option A is to administer intravenous hydrocortisone. Would that be a good choice?\n\nProfessor: Hydrocortisone is a glucocorticoid, which is one of the hormones that the adrenal glands produce. In Addison's disease, the adrenal glands are not producing enough of these hormones, so administering hydrocortisone could help alleviate some of the patient's symptoms. However, we should first confirm the diagnosis before starting treatment. Let's keep this option in mind and move on to the next one.\n\nStudent: Option B is to administer intravenous fludrocortisone. What about that?\n\nProfessor: Fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid, another hormone produced by the adrenal glands. It helps regulate the balance of salt and water in the body. While it could also be helpful in treating Addison's disease, we still need to confirm the diagnosis before starting treatment. Let's move on to the next option.\n\nStudent: Option C is to start him on androgen replacement treatment. Would that be appropriate?\n\nProfessor: Androgens are another class of hormones produced by the adrenal glands. However, they are not typically involved in the symptoms of Addison's disease, so starting androgen replacement treatment would not be the most appropriate next step. Let's move on to the next option.\n\nStudent: Option D is to start him on both hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone therapy. What do you think about that?\n\nProfessor: As we discussed earlier, both hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone could be helpful in treating Addison's disease. However, we still need to confirm the diagnosis before starting treatment. Let's look at the last option.\n\nStudent: Option E is to order an ACTH stimulation test",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "3669602d-7846-4156-a547-f030a52be296",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) Order an ACTH stimulation test.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! The ACTH stimulation test is used to diagnose Addison's disease by measuring how well the adrenal glands respond to adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). If the patient has Addison's disease, his adrenal glands will not produce enough cortisol in response to ACTH. By ordering this test, we can confirm the diagnosis before starting any treatment. So, the next best step in the management of this patient is to order an ACTH stimulation test.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
