                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The hospital would use a Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) assay to detect the genetic material of HIV if the child had been infected. PCR is a technique used to amplify a specific segment of DNA, and it can detect very small amounts of viral genetic material in a sample. In this case, the PCR assay would be used to test a sample of the baby's blood or other bodily fluids for the presence of HIV genetic material. ELISA, Western blot, and Rapid HIV antibody tests are all used to detect antibodies to HIV in a person's blood, which would not be present in a newborn who has not yet developed their own immune system. Southern blot is a technique used to detect specific DNA sequences, but it is not commonly used for HIV testing.",
