                    {
                        "id": "895b8493-f631-4ec5-97e5-947b216915b5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What assay would a hospital use to detect the genetic material of HIV in a newborn baby if the mother was HIV positive and did not consistently take her anti-retroviral medications during pregnancy?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The question is asking about an assay that can detect the genetic material of HIV in a newborn baby.\n- The baby's mother is HIV positive and did not consistently take her anti-retroviral medications during pregnancy.\n- This puts the baby at risk of being infected with HIV.\n- Therefore, the hospital needs to test the baby for HIV.\n- The question is asking which assay would be used for this purpose.\n\nAnswer: D) Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is the assay that a hospital would use to detect the genetic material of HIV in a newborn baby if the mother was HIV positive and did not consistently take her anti-retroviral medications during pregnancy. PCR is a highly sensitive and specific test that can detect even small amounts of HIV genetic material in a sample. ELISA, Western blot, and rapid HIV antibody tests are all used to detect antibodies to HIV, which may not be present in a newborn baby who has been recently infected. Southern blot is a technique used to detect specific DNA sequences, but it is not commonly used for HIV testing.",
                        "answers": [
