                    {
                        "id": "c7df24fc-3cbb-47cf-9a75-a168c27b7dcd",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, and can be transmitted from mother to child during pregnancy, childbirth, or breastfeeding. Anti-retroviral medications can reduce the risk of transmission, but haphazard use can increase the risk. Assays are laboratory tests used to detect the presence of HIV in blood or other bodily fluids.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the purpose of an assay in detecting HIV?\n2. How does each of the listed assays work?\n3. Which assay is most effective at detecting the genetic material of HIV?\n4. Would the presence of HIV antibodies in the child's blood necessarily indicate infection?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The purpose of an assay in detecting HIV is to identify the presence of the virus in blood or other bodily fluids. This can be done by detecting either the virus itself or the antibodies produced by the body in response to the virus.\n2. ELISA, Western blot, and rapid HIV antibody tests all detect HIV antibodies in the blood. Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) detects the genetic material (RNA) of the virus, while Southern blot is a technique used to detect specific DNA sequences.\n3. PCR is the most effective assay at detecting the genetic material of HIV, as it can detect even small amounts of the virus in the blood.\n4. Not necessarily. HIV antibodies can be present in the blood of a child born to an HIV-positive mother, even if the child is not infected. This is because the antibodies can be passed from the mother to the child during pregnancy or breastfeeding. Therefore, a positive antibody test would need to be followed up with a PCR test to confirm infection. \n\nAnswer to original question: The hospital would use a Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) assay to detect the genetic material of HIV if the child had been infected.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "96d74f9d-f245-42b3-a3cd-bb86aed1eded",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Polymerase chain reaction.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/30 03:56:05",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
