                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which assay would be used to detect the genetic material of HIV in a newborn baby if the baby had been infected.\n\nStep 2: Review the answer choices\nA) Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) - This test is used to detect the presence of antibodies against HIV in a person's blood. However, it may not be suitable for detecting HIV in newborns, as they may have maternal antibodies present.\nB) Western blot - This test is used to confirm the presence of HIV antibodies after a positive ELISA test. It is not used to detect the genetic material of HIV.\nC) Rapid HIV antibody test - This test is similar to ELISA and detects HIV antibodies in a person's blood. It is not used to detect the genetic material of HIV.\nD) Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) - This test is used to detect the genetic material of HIV (RNA or DNA) in a person's blood. It is suitable for detecting HIV in newborns, as it does not rely on the presence of antibodies.\nE) Southern blot - This test is used to detect specific DNA sequences in a sample. It is not commonly used to detect HIV genetic material in clinical settings.\n\nStep 3: Choose the correct answer\nThe correct answer is D) Polymerase chain reaction (PCR), as it is used to detect the genetic material of HIV and is suitable for detecting HIV in newborns.",
