                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Answer: D) Polymerase chain reaction\n\nCritique: The answer is correct because the polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a technique used to amplify and detect specific DNA sequences, such as the genetic material of HIV. PCR is highly sensitive and can detect the presence of HIV in a newborn's blood, even if the virus is present in very low amounts.\n\nReconsidering other options:\nA) Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) - This test detects antibodies against HIV, not the genetic material of the virus itself. It may not be accurate in newborns, as they can have maternal antibodies that can cause false-positive results.\nB) Western blot - This test also detects antibodies against HIV and has the same limitations as ELISA in newborns.\nC) Rapid HIV antibody test - This test detects HIV antibodies and has the same limitations as ELISA and Western blot in newborns.\nE) Southern blot - This technique detects specific DNA sequences, but it is less sensitive and more time-consuming than PCR.\n\nFinal answer: D) Polymerase chain reaction",
                        "answers": [
