                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The symptoms described suggest a neurological deficit affecting the left leg, which is dropping when the patient stands on the right leg. This suggests a problem with the nerves controlling the left leg, which could be related to the injection site of the vaccine. The most likely location for this injection site would be in the buttock, as this is a common site for intramuscular injections.\n\nTo determine the specific location of the injection site, we need to consider the nerve pathways that could be affected. The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body and runs down the back of the leg, branching into the tibial and peroneal nerves at the knee. Damage to the sciatic nerve or its branches could cause weakness or paralysis of the muscles in the leg, as well as altered sensation.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms, we can infer that the injection site is affecting the left sciatic nerve or its branches. This would suggest that the injection was given in the right buttock, as the sciatic nerve runs down the back of the leg and is more likely to be affected by an injection in the opposite buttock.\n\nOf the options given, the most likely location for the injection site is the inferolateral quadrant of the right buttock, as this is closest to the path of the sciatic nerve. Therefore, the correct answer is A) Inferolateral quadrant of the right buttock.",
