                        "cot": "Question: In a 5-year-old boy with an irregular gait after receiving vaccinations, where was the vaccine most likely injected if his left leg drops and pelvis tilts towards the left when standing on his right leg?\n\nStep 1: Identify the affected muscles and nerves\nThe symptoms described (left leg dropping and pelvis tilting towards the left when standing on the right leg) suggest that the patient's gluteus medius and minimus muscles are affected. These muscles are responsible for stabilizing the pelvis during single-leg stance. The superior gluteal nerve innervates these muscles.\n\nStep 2: Determine the location of the injection that could affect the superior gluteal nerve\nThe superior gluteal nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, above the piriformis muscle, and runs between the gluteus medius and minimus muscles. An injection in the upper part of the buttock, particularly in the superomedial quadrant, could potentially damage the superior gluteal nerve.\n\nStep 3: Match the injection location with the answer choices\nThe injection location that could affect the superior gluteal nerve and cause the patient's symptoms is the superomedial quadrant of the right buttock.\n\nAnswer: D) Superomedial quadrant of the right buttock",
                        "answers": [
