                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely diagnosis for a 65-year-old man with back pain, numbness, and weakness in his legs, who has a history of osteoporosis, dyslipidemia, diabetes, drinks 3 alcoholic drinks every day, and has a 44 pack-year smoking history?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient has gradually worsening back pain that worsened after moving furniture, and feels numbness and weakness in his legs while walking. This suggests a neurological component to the pain.\n- The patient's back pain is improved when he is riding his bike or pushing a cart at the grocery store. This suggests that the pain is relieved by activities that involve flexion of the spine.\n- The patient has a history of osteoporosis, which increases the risk of compression fractures in the spine.\n- The patient's blood pressure is elevated, which could be a contributing factor to the back pain.\n- The patient's physical exam is notable for a non-tender spine with normal mobility in all 4 directions, which suggests that there is no musculoskeletal strain or herniated nucleus pulposus causing the pain.\n- The patient's basic labs and radiography of the spine are pending, so we cannot use those results to make a diagnosis.\n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis for this patient is A) Compression fracture, given his history of osteoporosis and the nature of his back pain.",
