                    {
                        "id": "b62bca16-7870-472c-ade2-ab0a78ef75d5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what a compression fracture is?\n\nProfessor: A compression fracture is a type of fracture that occurs when a vertebra in the spine collapses or is compressed. This can happen due to osteoporosis, trauma, or other underlying conditions.\n\nStudent: How about a herniated nucleus pulposus?\n\nProfessor: A herniated nucleus pulposus, also known as a herniated disc, occurs when the soft center of a spinal disc pushes through a crack in the tougher exterior. This can cause pressure on nearby nerves and result in pain, numbness, or weakness in the affected area.\n\nStudent: What is musculoskeletal strain?\n\nProfessor: Musculoskeletal strain is a type of injury that occurs when a muscle or tendon is stretched or torn. This can happen due to overuse, improper lifting, or sudden movements.\n\nStudent: Can you explain spinal stenosis?\n\nProfessor: Spinal stenosis is a condition where the spinal canal narrows and puts pressure on the spinal cord and nerves. This can cause pain, numbness, or weakness in the affected area.\n\nStudent: And what is vascular claudication?\n\nProfessor: Vascular claudication is a condition where there is decreased blood flow to the legs, usually due to atherosclerosis or narrowing of the blood vessels. This can cause pain or cramping in the legs during physical activity.\n\nStudent: Based on the patient's symptoms and medical history, which of these is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nProfessor: Given the patient's history of osteoporosis and recent activity that may have put stress on the spine, as well as his symptoms of numbness and weakness in the legs, the most likely diagnosis is a compression fracture. However, we will need to wait for the results of the radiography and labs to confirm the diagnosis.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "a7bdc04d-1a3e-40a0-83ec-01c6c91972df",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Compression fracture.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "A",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
