                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, we need to identify the patient's symptoms and medical history. The patient is a 35-year-old woman who has sudden weakness on her right arm and leg, has a fever, and has high blood pressure. She has a history of smoking and is taking an oral contraceptive. She also has swelling, redness, and tenderness on her left lower leg.\n\nNext, we need to consider the possible causes of her symptoms. Given her recent travel to Europe, we should consider the possibility of a blood clot (deep vein thrombosis) in her leg that has traveled to her brain and caused a stroke. This could also explain her fever and high blood pressure. Other possible causes of stroke include a patent foramen ovale (a hole in the heart), atrial myxoma (a tumor in the heart), mitral valve vegetation (infection of the heart valve), and carotid artery dissection (tear in the artery that supplies blood to the brain).\n\nBased on her symptoms and medical history, the most likely cause of her stroke is a blood clot from a deep vein thrombosis in her leg. This is supported by the swelling, redness, and tenderness in her left lower leg. Therefore, the answer is E) Carotid artery dissection.",
