                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 35-year-old woman with sudden weakness of her right arm and leg that started 3 hours ago. She returned from a business trip from Europe 3 days ago. She has smoked a pack of cigarettes daily for 10 years and is on an oral contraceptive. Her vital signs show a fever of 38.0\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F), tachycardia with a pulse of 115/min and regular, and hypertension with a blood pressure of 155/85 mm Hg. Examination shows decreased muscle strength on the entire right side, 4+ deep tendon reflexes on the right, and a positive Babinski sign on the right. The left lower leg is swollen, erythematous, and tender to palpation. \n\nThe sudden onset of weakness on one side of the body, along with the presence of a fever, tachycardia, hypertension, and a swollen, tender, and erythematous leg suggests a possible diagnosis of stroke or deep vein thrombosis (DVT). The presence of a positive Babinski sign on the right suggests an upper motor neuron lesion, which is consistent with a stroke. \n\nOf the answer choices, the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is a DVT, which can lead to a pulmonary embolism (PE) and stroke. The swollen, tender, and erythematous left lower leg is a classic sign of DVT. The other answer choices are less likely to be the cause of the patient's symptoms. \n\nA) Patent foramen ovale is a congenital heart defect that can cause a stroke, but it is less likely in this case because the patient has no history of heart problems and her symptoms are more consistent with a DVT. \nB) Atrial myxoma is a rare tumor of the heart that can cause stroke, but it is less likely in this case because the patient has no history of heart problems and her symptoms are more consistent with a DVT. \nC) Mitral valve vegetation is a growth on the heart valve that can cause stroke, but it is less likely in this case because the patient has no history of heart problems and her symptoms are more consistent with a DVT. \nD) Atrial fibrillation is an irregular heart rhythm that can cause stroke, but it is less likely in this case because the patient has no history of heart problems and her symptoms are more consistent with a DVT. \nE) Carotid artery dissection is a tear in the",
