                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 35-year-old woman who has sudden weakness of her right arm and leg, which started 3 hours ago. She has a history of smoking and takes an oral contraceptive. She has a fever, tachycardia, and hypertension. Examination shows decreased muscle strength on the entire right side, 4+ deep tendon reflexes on the right, and a positive Babinski sign on the right. The left lower leg is swollen, erythematous, and tender to palpation. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely diagnosis for the patient's symptoms?\n2. What is the significance of the patient's history of smoking and taking an oral contraceptive?\n3. What is the significance of the patient's fever, tachycardia, and hypertension?\n4. What is the significance of the patient's decreased muscle strength on the entire right side, 4+ deep tendon reflexes on the right, and positive Babinski sign on the right?\n5. What is the significance of the patient's left lower leg swelling, erythema, and tenderness?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The most likely diagnosis for the patient's symptoms is a stroke, specifically an ischemic stroke, which is caused by a blockage in a blood vessel in the brain.\n2. The patient's history of smoking and taking an oral contraceptive increases her risk for stroke.\n3. The patient's fever, tachycardia, and hypertension may be signs of an infection or inflammation, which could be a contributing factor to the stroke.\n4. The patient's decreased muscle strength on the entire right side, 4+ deep tendon reflexes on the right, and positive Babinski sign on the right are all signs of a neurological deficit, which is consistent with a stroke.\n5. The patient's left lower leg swelling, erythema, and tenderness may be a sign of deep vein thrombosis (DVT), which is a risk factor for stroke. \n\nTherefore, the most likely answer is E) Carotid artery dissection, as this is a common cause of stroke in young patients with a history of smoking and taking an oral contraceptive. However, further evaluation may also reveal the presence of DVT, which would also be a contributing factor to the stroke. The other answer choices (A) Patent foramen ovale, (B) Atrial myxoma, (C) Mitral valve vegetation, and (D) Atrial fibrillation, are less likely given the patient's clinical",
