                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe most likely diagnosis based on the patient's symptoms and physical examination is E) Carotid artery dissection. Carotid artery dissection is a tear in the wall of the carotid artery, which can cause sudden weakness of one side of the body, as well as swelling and tenderness in the lower leg. The patient's history of smoking and recent travel to Europe are both risk factors for carotid artery dissection. The patient's temperature, pulse, and blood pressure are all within normal limits, which suggests that the patient does not have an infection or other systemic illness. The presence of Babinski sign on the right side is also suggestive of carotid artery dissection. The other options listed are not likely to be the cause of the patient's symptoms.",
