                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what ascites is?\n\nProfessor: Ascites is the accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity. It can be caused by a variety of conditions, including liver disease, heart failure, and cancer.\n\nStudent: What is hepatojugular reflux?\n\nProfessor: Hepatojugular reflux is a physical exam finding where pressure is applied to the liver and the jugular vein is observed for distention. If the jugular vein distends, it suggests that there is increased pressure in the right side of the heart, which can be caused by heart failure or other conditions.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what a systolic murmur is?\n\nProfessor: A systolic murmur is a type of heart murmur that occurs during the contraction of the heart. It is caused by turbulent blood flow through the heart valves and can be indicative of a variety of conditions, including valve disease and heart failure.\n\nStudent: What is an echocardiogram?\n\nProfessor: An echocardiogram is a non-invasive test that uses sound waves to create images of the heart. It can be used to evaluate the structure and function of the heart, including the size and shape of the chambers, the thickness of the heart walls, and the movement of the heart valves. \n\nStudent: So, which of the following is the most likely clinical finding observed in this patient on an echocardiogram?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is B) Increased peak tricuspid regurgitation. Tricuspid regurgitation is a condition where blood flows backwards through the tricuspid valve, which separates the right atrium and right ventricle. It can be caused by a variety of conditions, including heart failure. An increase in peak tricuspid regurgitation on an echocardiogram would suggest that the patient has developed right-sided heart failure.",
