                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe most likely clinical finding observed in this patient on an echocardiogram is C) Hypokinetic wall of the left ventricle.\n\nThis patient has a history of myocardial infarction 4 years ago, and is currently taking aspirin. He also has a 24-pack-year history of smoking and consumes alcohol occasionally. His vital signs include a blood pressure of 150/90 mm Hg, heart rate of 83/min, respiratory rate of 16/min, and temperature of 36.5\u2103 (97.7\u2109). On physical examination, the patient is pale and acrocyanotic, with a visible jugular vein distention and bilateral lower leg pitting edema. The pulmonary auscultation is significant for occasional bilateral wheezes, and cardiac auscultation is significant for a decreased S1, S3 gallop, and grade 3/6 systolic murmur best heard at the left sternal border in the 4th left intercostal space. Abdominal percussion and palpation are suggestive of ascites, and the hepatic margin is 3 cm below the right costal margin. Hepatojugular reflux is positive.\n\nGiven the patient's history and physical examination findings, the most likely clinical finding observed in this patient on an echocardiogram is a hypokinetic wall of the left ventricle. This is due to the patient's history of myocardial infarction, which can lead to left ventricular dysfunction and a hypokinetic wall. The other options (A) Left ventricular ejection fraction of 41%, (B) Increased peak tricuspid regurgitation, (D) Abnormal left ventricular relaxation, and (E) Decreased right atrial pressure are less likely to be observed in this patient.",
