                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: In a 59-year-old man with sickle cell disease who presents with fever, night sweats, lethargy, and swollen bite marks on his right hand, which of the following bites would be the most concerning?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient has sickle cell disease, which puts him at risk for infections and complications.\n- He has fever, night sweats, and is lethargic, which are signs of infection.\n- His wife reported that he was bitten by something, but she cannot remember what it was.\n- The patient has swollen bite marks on his right hand with red streaks extending to his elbow, which suggests an infection that is spreading.\n- The patient's vital signs are concerning, with low blood pressure, high pulse rate, and high respiratory rate.\n- The patient's spleen is surgically absent, which puts him at higher risk for infections.\n- Therefore, the most concerning bite in this patient would be one that is likely to cause a severe infection that can spread quickly.\n\nAnswer: C) Spider bite. Spider bites can cause severe infections that can spread quickly, especially in patients with compromised immune systems. The swollen bite marks and red streaks suggest that the infection is spreading, and the patient's vital signs are concerning. Cat, human, fish, and dog bites can also cause infections, but they are less likely to cause severe infections that spread quickly.",
