                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: In a 59-year-old man with a history of sickle cell disease and fever, night sweats, lethargy, and swollen bite marks on his right hand with red streaks extending to his elbow, which type of bite would be most concerning?\nA) Cat bite\nB) Human bite\nC) Spider bite\nD) Fish bite\nE) Dog bite\n\nStep 1: Consider the patient's history and symptoms\nThe patient has a history of sickle cell disease, which can cause an increased risk of infection due to a compromised immune system. He is presenting with fever, night sweats, lethargy, and swollen bite marks with red streaks, which could indicate an infection.\n\nStep 2: Evaluate the potential risks associated with each type of bite\nA) Cat bites can introduce bacteria into the body and cause infection, especially if not treated promptly.\nB) Human bites can also introduce bacteria and cause infection, and are often considered more dangerous than animal bites due to the types of bacteria present in the human mouth.\nC) Spider bites can cause localized pain, swelling, and redness, but most are not dangerous. However, some spider bites can cause severe symptoms and require medical attention.\nD) Fish bites are generally not dangerous, but can cause infection if not treated properly.\nE) Dog bites can also introduce bacteria and cause infection, and are a common cause of bite-related injuries.\n\nStep 3: Determine which bite would be most concerning for this patient\nGiven the patient's history of sickle cell disease and increased risk of infection, the most concerning bite would be one that has a high likelihood of causing infection. Human bites are often considered more dangerous than animal bites due to the types of bacteria present in the human mouth. Therefore, the most concerning bite in this patient would be:\n\nB) Human bite",
