                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: What is the next step in the management of a 55-year-old man with a history of a heart attack, difficulty breathing, a severe cough, and regular alcohol consumption, who has an ECG and chest X-ray available?\n\nFirst, let's consider the patient's symptoms and history. He has difficulty breathing, especially at night, and a persistent cough. He also has a history of a heart attack and continues to consume alcohol despite medical advice. These factors suggest that the patient may have a cardiac issue or a pulmonary issue.\n\nStep 1: Review the available tests - ECG and chest X-ray. These tests can provide information about the patient's heart and lung function. If there are any abnormalities in these tests, it may help guide the next step in management.\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible next steps in management. The options are angiography, CT, echocardiogram, stress test, and cardiac biopsy.\n\nA) Angiography: This test involves injecting a dye into the blood vessels and taking X-ray images to visualize the blood flow in the heart. It can help diagnose blockages in the coronary arteries. However, it is an invasive procedure and may not be the best first step in this case.\n\nB) Computed Tomography (CT): A CT scan can provide detailed images of the chest, including the heart and lungs. It may help identify any structural abnormalities or other issues. However, it may not provide enough information about the heart's function.\n\nC) Echocardiogram: This test uses ultrasound to create images of the heart and assess its function. It can help diagnose heart failure, valve problems, and other cardiac issues. Given the patient's history and symptoms, this seems like a reasonable next step.\n\nD) Stress test: This test involves monitoring the heart's function during exercise. It can help diagnose coronary artery disease and other heart problems. However, given the patient's difficulty breathing and need to rest after a few steps, this may not be the most appropriate test at this time.\n\nE) Cardiac biopsy: This involves taking a small sample of heart tissue for examination. It is an invasive procedure and is typically reserved for cases where other tests have not provided a clear diagnosis.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms, history, and available tests, the next step in this patient's management should be an echocardiogram (C). This test can help assess the heart's function and identify any underlying cardiac issues that may be contributing to the patient's symptoms.",
                        "answers": [
