                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: What is the most likely cause of a 2-month-old girl's symptoms, which include failure to gain weight, chronic diarrhea, multiple bacterial and viral infections, an absent thymic shadow, and the absence of germinal centers in a lymph node biopsy?\n\nStep 1: Analyze the symptoms and findings\n- Failure to gain weight\n- Chronic diarrhea\n- Multiple bacterial and viral infections\n- Absent thymic shadow\n- Absence of germinal centers in lymph node biopsy\n\nStep 2: Evaluate the possible causes\nA) Defect in WASP gene: This would cause Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, which is characterized by eczema, thrombocytopenia, and immunodeficiency. However, it does not typically cause an absent thymic shadow or absence of germinal centers.\nB) Defect in ATM gene: This would cause ataxia-telangiectasia, which is characterized by progressive cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, and immunodeficiency. However, it does not typically cause an absent thymic shadow or absence of germinal centers.\nC) Adenosine deaminase deficiency: This would cause severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), which is characterized by a lack of both T and B cells, leading to severe infections, failure to thrive, and chronic diarrhea. This is consistent with the patient's symptoms and findings.\nD) NADPH oxidase deficiency: This would cause chronic granulomatous disease, which is characterized by recurrent infections and granuloma formation. However, it does not typically cause an absent thymic shadow or absence of germinal centers.\nE) Defect in BTK gene: This would cause X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which is characterized by a lack of B cells and recurrent infections. However, it does not typically cause an absent thymic shadow or absence of germinal centers.\n\nStep 3: Determine the most likely cause\nBased on the patient's symptoms and findings, the most likely cause is adenosine deaminase deficiency (C), as it is consistent with the severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) presentation.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "777325e4-b442-4cc5-9434-e2eb7d4d20ef",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/12 21:54:03",
