                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to first understand the role of each gene mentioned in the options and how their defects can lead to the symptoms presented by the patient. Then, we can determine which gene defect is most likely causing the patient's symptoms.\n\nA) Defect in WASP gene: Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome Protein (WASP) gene is involved in the immune system's response to infections. A defect in this gene can lead to Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome, which is characterized by recurrent infections, eczema, and thrombocytopenia (low platelet count). This does not fully match the patient's symptoms.\n\nB) Defect in ATM gene: Ataxia-telangiectasia mutated (ATM) gene is involved in DNA repair and cell cycle regulation. A defect in this gene can lead to Ataxia-telangiectasia, which is characterized by progressive neurological problems, immune system abnormalities, and increased risk of cancer. This does not fully match the patient's symptoms.\n\nC) Adenosine deaminase deficiency: Adenosine deaminase (ADA) is an enzyme involved in purine metabolism. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID), which is characterized by recurrent infections, failure to thrive, and chronic diarrhea. This matches the patient's symptoms.\n\nD) NADPH oxidase deficiency: NADPH oxidase is an enzyme involved in the production of reactive oxygen species, which are important for the immune system's response to infections. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to chronic granulomatous disease, which is characterized by recurrent infections and granuloma formation. This does not fully match the patient's symptoms.\n\nE) Defect in BTK gene: Bruton's tyrosine kinase (BTK) gene is involved in B cell development. A defect in this gene can lead to X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which is characterized by recurrent infections and low levels of immunoglobulins. This does not fully match the patient's symptoms.\n\nBased on the information above, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is:\n\nC) Adenosine deaminase deficiency",
                        "answers": [
