                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient's symptoms include failure to gain weight, chronic diarrhea, multiple bacterial and viral infections, absent thymic shadow, and absence of germinal centers in the lymph node biopsy. These findings suggest a severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID) disorder.\n\nA) Defect in WASP gene: This would cause Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome, which is characterized by eczema, thrombocytopenia, and recurrent infections. This does not match the patient's presentation.\n\nB) Defect in ATM gene: This would cause ataxia-telangiectasia, which is characterized by progressive cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, and immunodeficiency. This does not match the patient's presentation.\n\nC) Adenosine deaminase deficiency: This is a cause of SCID, which matches the patient's presentation. Adenosine deaminase deficiency leads to the accumulation of toxic metabolites, causing the death of both T and B cells, resulting in severe combined immunodeficiency.\n\nD) NADPH oxidase deficiency: This would cause chronic granulomatous disease, which is characterized by recurrent bacterial and fungal infections and granuloma formation. This does not match the patient's presentation.\n\nE) Defect in BTK gene: This would cause X-linked agammaglobulinemia, which is characterized by recurrent bacterial infections and very low levels of immunoglobulins. This does not match the patient's presentation.\n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is C) Adenosine deaminase deficiency.",
