                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Metabolic alkalosis\n\nThe patient\u2019s presentation is consistent with pyloric stenosis, a condition in which the muscle at the bottom of the stomach (pylorus) thickens and narrows, leading to obstruction of the gastric outlet. The persistent vomiting leads to loss of hydrochloric acid (HCl) from the stomach, resulting in metabolic alkalosis. The loss of HCl leads to a decrease in the concentration of hydrogen ions (H+) in the blood, causing an increase in pH and bicarbonate (HCO3-) levels. The other answer choices are not associated with pyloric stenosis. Respiratory alkalosis is characterized by hyperventilation and a decrease in carbon dioxide (CO2) levels, which is not seen in this patient. Metabolic acidosis is characterized by a decrease in pH and bicarbonate levels, which is not seen in this patient. Respiratory acidosis is characterized by an increase in CO2 levels and a decrease in pH, which is not seen in this patient. Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis is a combination of respiratory and metabolic acidosis, which is not seen in this patient.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "842e3fb1-a4cd-42cd-b6ea-5f76098efbd4",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
