                    {
                        "id": "068313a4-83fe-4e89-aade-b202f1ac79f2",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 1-month-old girl with persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability. She has been projectile vomiting after most feedings, and now it occurs after every feed. Her appetite hasn\u2019t decreased, and she seeks milk right after each episode of vomiting. On physical exam, she appears to be mildly dehydrated, listless, and irritable. Her abdomen is soft and nontender with a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region. \n\nThe olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region is suggestive of pyloric stenosis, a condition in which the pylorus, the muscular valve that regulates the flow of food from the stomach to the small intestine, becomes thickened and narrowed, leading to obstruction of the gastric outlet. This obstruction causes the stomach to become distended, leading to persistent vomiting, dehydration, and electrolyte imbalances. \n\nThe persistent vomiting and dehydration can lead to metabolic alkalosis, a condition in which there is an excess of bicarbonate in the blood. This occurs because the loss of gastric acid in the vomitus leads to a decrease in hydrogen ion concentration, which stimulates the kidneys to reabsorb bicarbonate. The compensatory mechanism for metabolic alkalosis is hypoventilation, which leads to an increase in carbon dioxide retention and respiratory acidosis. \n\nTherefore, the most likely acid-base disturbance associated with this patient\u2019s condition is C) Metabolic alkalosis.",
                        "answers": [
