                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What acid-base disturbance is most likely associated with a 1-month-old girl with persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability, and a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region?\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. The patient is a 1-month-old girl with persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability.\n2. She has been projectile vomiting after most feedings for the past week, and now it occurs after every feed.\n3. Her appetite hasn't decreased, and she seeks milk right after each episode of vomiting.\n4. She was born at 39 weeks gestation via spontaneous vaginal delivery and is breastfed exclusively.\n5. On physical exam, she appears to be mildly dehydrated, listless, and irritable.\n6. Her abdomen is soft and nontender with a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region.\n\nTherefore, the acid-base disturbance most likely associated with this patient's condition is C) Metabolic alkalosis. The persistent vomiting has caused a loss of acid from the stomach, leading to an increase in serum bicarbonate concentration and metabolic alkalosis. The palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region is suggestive of pyloric stenosis, which is a common cause of persistent vomiting in infants.",
