                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is a 1-month-old girl who is exclusively breastfed and has been experiencing persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability. On physical exam, there is a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region?\n2. What are the possible causes of persistent vomiting in a 1-month-old girl?\n3. What are the signs and symptoms of dehydration?\n4. What are the different types of acid-base disturbances?\n5. Which acid-base disturbance is most likely associated with the patient's condition?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region is likely to be a hypertrophied pylorus, which is a thickening of the muscle at the outlet of the stomach.\n2. Possible causes of persistent vomiting in a 1-month-old girl include pyloric stenosis, gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), milk protein allergy, and infections such as gastroenteritis.\n3. Signs and symptoms of dehydration include dry mouth and tongue, sunken eyes, decreased urine output, lethargy, and irritability.\n4. The different types of acid-base disturbances are respiratory acidosis, respiratory alkalosis, metabolic acidosis, and metabolic alkalosis.\n5. The most likely acid-base disturbance associated with the patient's condition is metabolic alkalosis. Pyloric stenosis can cause vomiting, which leads to loss of hydrochloric acid (HCl) from the stomach. The loss of HCl leads to an increase in the pH of the blood, resulting in metabolic alkalosis.",
