                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what is happening with the baby in this scenario?\n\nProfessor: The baby is experiencing persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability. She has been projectile vomiting after most feedings, and now it occurs after every feed. However, her appetite hasn\u2019t decreased, and she seeks milk right after each episode of vomiting. On physical exam, she appears to be mildly dehydrated, listless, and irritable. Her abdomen is soft and nontender with a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of the olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region?\n\nProfessor: The olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region is a key finding in this scenario. It suggests that the baby may have pyloric stenosis, which is a condition where the muscle at the bottom of the stomach (pylorus) becomes thickened and prevents food from passing into the small intestine.\n\nStudent: How does pyloric stenosis lead to acid-base disturbances?\n\nProfessor: Pyloric stenosis can lead to acid-base disturbances because it causes persistent vomiting, which can result in the loss of stomach acid and electrolytes. This can lead to metabolic alkalosis, which is characterized by an increase in pH and bicarbonate levels in the blood.\n\nStudent: So, the answer is C) Metabolic alkalosis?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that is correct. The most likely acid-base disturbance associated with this patient\u2019s condition is metabolic alkalosis.",
