                    {
                        "id": "fc4d5fd0-48c7-4897-9058-1dc271c81a20",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nFirst, it is important to note that the patient is a 1-month-old girl who is breastfed exclusively and has been projectile vomiting after most feedings for the past week. Additionally, her vital signs include a temperature of 37.5\u00b0C (99.5\u00b0F), blood pressure of 85/55 mm Hg, respiratory rate of 28/min, and heart rate of 150/min. On physical exam, she appears to be mildly dehydrated, listless, and irritable, with a soft and nontender abdomen and a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the cause of the patient's vomiting?\n2. What is the cause of the patient's acid-base disturbance?\n\n1. The cause of the patient's vomiting is likely related to the olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region. This could be a sign of pyloric stenosis, which is a narrowing of the pylorus, the lower part of the stomach that connects to the small intestine. Pyloric stenosis can cause projectile vomiting after feedings due to the obstruction of the stomach outlet.\n\n2. The acid-base disturbance associated with this patient's condition is likely metabolic acidosis. Metabolic acidosis is caused by an accumulation of acid in the body, which can be caused by dehydration, vomiting, and/or diarrhea. In this case, the patient's dehydration, vomiting, and irritability are all signs of metabolic acidosis. Therefore, the answer is B) Metabolic acidosis.",
                        "answers": [
