                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step: \n\n1) She is admitted to the labor and delivery suite with mild abdominal cramps, uterine contractions, and a watery vaginal discharge. \n2) She has a history of preterm birth. \n3) The vital signs are as follows: blood pressure 125/80 mm Hg; heart rate 100/min; respiratory rate 13/min; and temperature 36.6\u2103 (97.9\u2109). \n4) The pelvic examination reveals cervical softening and shortening. \n5) Transvaginal ultrasound shows a cervical length of 12 mm, which is consistent with preterm labor. \n6) A tocolytic and a single dose of betamethasone are administered.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis is preterm labor. Betamethasone stimulates fetal cells, which are necessary for the development of the lungs.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "",
