                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: Betamethasone stimulates which fetal cells in a 26-year-old gravida 2 para 1 at 24 weeks gestation who is experiencing preterm labor?\n\nStep 1: Understand the situation\nA 26-year-old pregnant woman is admitted to the hospital with signs of preterm labor. She is given a tocolytic and a single dose of betamethasone. We need to determine which fetal cells betamethasone stimulates.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options\nA) Type I pneumocytes: These cells are involved in gas exchange in the lungs.\nB) Goblet cells: These cells produce mucus in the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts.\nC) Bronchial epithelial cells: These cells line the bronchi in the lungs.\nD) Type II pneumocytes: These cells produce surfactant, which reduces surface tension in the lungs and prevents alveolar collapse.\nE) Vascular smooth myocytes: These cells are involved in the contraction and relaxation of blood vessels.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBetamethasone is a corticosteroid that is administered to pregnant women at risk of preterm labor to help accelerate fetal lung maturation. The primary effect of betamethasone is to stimulate the production of surfactant, which is produced by Type II pneumocytes. Therefore, the correct answer is D) Type II pneumocytes.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "2d90b12f-78f4-4c67-aca5-d09a77954cec",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
