                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with a cough, fatigue, confusion, fever (102\u00b0F), tachycardia (pulse 120/min), and crackles on pulmonary exam. These symptoms suggest a possible respiratory infection, such as pneumonia. Given the patient's age, comorbidities (diabetes and hypertension), and nursing home residence, he is at higher risk for complications from pneumonia.\n\nThe next best step in management would be to start appropriate antibiotic therapy and admit the patient to the hospital for monitoring and further treatment.\n\nOption A (Azithromycin and admission to the medical floor) provides only one antibiotic, which may not be sufficient to cover the potential pathogens causing the patient's pneumonia.\n\nOption B (Azithromycin and discharge) is not appropriate, as the patient's symptoms and risk factors warrant hospital admission for monitoring and treatment.\n\nOption C (Azithromycin, moxifloxacin, and admission to the intensive care unit) provides two antibiotics, which may be more appropriate for the patient's potential pneumonia. However, admitting the patient to the intensive care unit may not be necessary at this point, as his vital signs are relatively stable (oxygen saturation 98% on room air, blood pressure 107/58 mmHg).\n\nOption D (Ceftriaxone, azithromycin, and admission to the intensive care unit) also provides two antibiotics, but again, admitting the patient to the intensive care unit may not be necessary at this point.\n\nOption E (Moxifloxacin and admission to the medical floor) provides only one antibiotic, which may not be sufficient to cover the potential pathogens causing the patient's pneumonia.\n\nBased on the information provided, the most appropriate choice would be option C (Azithromycin, moxifloxacin, and admission to the intensive care unit). Although the patient's vital signs are relatively stable, his age, comorbidities, and nursing home residence put him at higher risk for complications from pneumonia. Starting two antibiotics and admitting him to the intensive care unit would provide the most comprehensive care and monitoring for this patient.",
                        "answers": [
