                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: I'm not sure which option is the best next step in management for this patient. Can you help me work through this?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by considering the patient's symptoms and vital signs. What do they suggest to you?\n\nStudent: The patient has a cough, fatigue, and confusion. His temperature is elevated, and he has crackles on pulmonary exam and an S4 on cardiac auscultation. This suggests that he might have an infection, possibly pneumonia.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Now, let's consider the different treatment options. What do you know about azithromycin?\n\nStudent: Azithromycin is a macrolide antibiotic that can be used to treat community-acquired pneumonia.\n\nProfessor: Good. And what about moxifloxacin?\n\nStudent: Moxifloxacin is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic that can also be used to treat community-acquired pneumonia.\n\nProfessor: Excellent. Now, let's consider the patient's current condition and the different treatment options. What factors might influence our decision?\n\nStudent: The patient is elderly, has a history of diabetes and hypertension, and is currently taking multiple medications. He also has an elevated temperature and abnormal vital signs. These factors suggest that he might be at higher risk for complications and might need more aggressive treatment.\n\nProfessor: That's a good analysis. So, given the patient's condition and the available treatment options, which option do you think is the best next step in management?\n\nStudent: I think option D, ceftriaxone, azithromycin, and admission to the intensive care unit, might be the best choice. This would provide broad-spectrum antibiotic coverage and allow for close monitoring of the patient's condition.\n\nProfessor: I agree. Option D is the best choice in this case. The combination of ceftriaxone and azithromycin provides appropriate coverage for the most likely pathogens causing community-acquired pneumonia, and admitting the patient to the intensive care unit allows for close monitoring and management of any complications that may arise. Well done!",
