                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings.\n2. Review the patient's lab results.\n3. Consider possible complications based on the patient's condition.\n4. Evaluate each answer choice and determine which complication is most likely.\n\nAnswer: E) Splenic sequestration crisis\n\nExplanation:\n1. The patient has a 3-day history of fatigue and yellow discoloration of his skin, indicating jaundice. He also has a palpable spleen tip 2 cm below the left costal margin.\n2. The patient's hemoglobin concentration is low at 9.4 g/dl and his mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is high at 39% Hb/cell.\n3. Based on the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings, the most likely complication is splenic sequestration crisis. This occurs when the spleen traps a large amount of blood, leading to a sudden drop in hemoglobin levels and potentially life-threatening anemia. This is a common complication in young children with sickle cell disease, which can cause splenic enlargement and dysfunction.\n4. The other answer choices are less likely based on the patient's condition. Malaria is a possibility in some cases of jaundice, but there is no evidence of infection in this patient. Acute myelogenous leukemia is unlikely given the patient's age and lack of other symptoms. Cholecystitis and renal papillary necrosis are also less likely given the patient's presentation.",
