                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 3-year-old boy with a 3-day history of fatigue and yellow discoloration of his skin. He had an upper respiratory tract infection one week ago. Examination shows jaundice of the skin and conjunctivae, and palpation reveals a spleen tip 2 cm below the left costal margin. His hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dl and his mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is 39% Hb/cell. A Coombs test is negative. A peripheral blood smear is shown. \n\nBased on the information provided, the patient is most likely experiencing a hemolytic anemia, as evidenced by the jaundice, splenomegaly, and low hemoglobin concentration. The peripheral blood smear may show evidence of hemolysis, such as schistocytes or spherocytes. The negative Coombs test suggests that the hemolysis is not immune-mediated.\n\nGiven the patient's age and clinical presentation, the most likely diagnosis is sickle cell anemia. Sickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder that causes abnormal hemoglobin to form, leading to the characteristic sickle-shaped red blood cells. These cells are more prone to hemolysis and can cause a variety of complications, including splenic sequestration crisis.\n\nTherefore, the patient is at greatest risk for splenic sequestration crisis (option E). This occurs when a large amount of blood becomes trapped in the spleen, leading to sudden enlargement of the spleen and a drop in hemoglobin concentration. This can be life-threatening and requires immediate medical attention.\n\nOptions A, B, C, and D are less likely in this patient based on the clinical presentation and peripheral blood smear. Malaria may cause hemolysis, but the patient's history and negative Coombs test make this less likely. Acute myelogenous leukemia and renal papillary necrosis are not associated with the clinical findings in this patient. Cholecystitis may cause jaundice, but the other findings are not consistent with this diagnosis.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "bddd3328-b44f-48d9-a968-d076d0c6ad55",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
