                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe patient is at greatest risk for Splenic Sequestration Crisis. This is because the patient has jaundice of the skin and conjunctivae, a hemoglobin concentration of 9.4 g/dl, and a mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration of 39% Hb/cell. These are all signs of anemia, which is a common symptom of splenic sequestration crisis. Additionally, the patient has a palpable spleen tip 2 cm below the left costal margin, which is another sign of splenic sequestration crisis. The Coombs test being negative is also indicative of splenic sequestration crisis. Therefore, the patient is at greatest risk for Splenic Sequestration Crisis.",
