                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nFirst, let's rephrase the question to make sure we understand it correctly: What is the greatest risk of complications for a 3-year-old boy with a 3-day history of fatigue and yellow discoloration of his skin, who had an upper respiratory tract infection one week ago, has jaundice of the skin and conjunctivae, a spleen tip palpated 2 cm below the left costal margin, a hemoglobin concentration of 9.4 g/dl, and a mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration of 39% Hb/cell, and a negative Coombs test?\n\nSecond, let's work this out step by step by spelling out our thoughts & reasoning so anyone can verify them: \nThe patient has jaundice, which is a symptom of anemia. Anemia is a condition in which the body does not have enough healthy red blood cells. The patient's hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dl, which is lower than the normal range of 11.5-15.5 g/dl. The patient's mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is 39% Hb/cell, which is also lower than the normal range of 32-36%. The Coombs test is negative, which means that the anemia is not caused by an autoimmune disorder. \n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and test results, the patient is at greatest risk for a splenic sequestration crisis, which is a complication of sickle cell anemia. Sickle cell anemia is an inherited disorder in which red blood cells become sickle-shaped and can get stuck in the spleen, leading to a splenic sequestration crisis.\n\nThird, make sure we have the right answer: The patient is at greatest risk for a splenic sequestration crisis (E).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "22e51ab0-019e-4bb5-b140-c64adcec067a",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
