                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nProfessor: Alright, let's break this down. What is the patient's age?\n\nStudent: He's 3 years old.\n\nProfessor: Okay, and what is the history of his symptoms?\n\nStudent: He's had fatigue and yellow discoloration of his skin for 3 days, and a week ago he had an upper respiratory tract infection.\n\nProfessor: Right. What does the examination show?\n\nStudent: Jaundice of the skin and conjunctivae, and the spleen tip is palpated 2 cm below the left costal margin.\n\nProfessor: Got it. What are the hemoglobin concentrations?\n\nStudent: His hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dl and his mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration is 39% Hb/cell.\n\nProfessor: And what does the Coombs test show?\n\nStudent: It's negative.\n\nProfessor: Alright. So, based on all this information, the patient is at greatest risk for which of the following complications?\n\nStudent: Is it A) Malaria, B) Acute myelogenous leukemia, C) Cholecystitis, D) Renal papillary necrosis, or E) Splenic sequestration crisis?\n\nProfessor: That's right. Based on the patient's age, symptoms, and examination results, the patient is at greatest risk for a splenic sequestration crisis.",
