                    {
                        "id": "445e7752-5774-49fd-b189-9c919d735778",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Acute myocardial infarction (AMI) is a medical emergency that occurs when blood flow to the heart muscle is blocked, leading to damage or death of the heart muscle cells. The diagnosis of AMI is based on clinical symptoms, electrocardiogram (ECG) changes, and biomarker elevations. Alteplase is a thrombolytic medication used to dissolve blood clots in patients with AMI.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the most likely cause of the patient's chest pain?\n2. What is the significance of the patient's vital signs and chest auscultation findings?\n3. What is the significance of the patient's difficult-to-interpret ECG?\n4. What laboratory tests are typically used to diagnose AMI?\n5. Which of the listed laboratory tests would have the most diagnostic utility in this patient?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The most likely cause of the patient's chest pain is a recurrent or ongoing AMI. The fact that the patient had an ST-elevation myocardial infarction 5 days ago and is now experiencing chest pain again suggests that there may be ongoing ischemia or reperfusion injury to the heart muscle.\n2. The patient's vital signs are within normal limits, but his blood pressure is slightly elevated. The absence of abnormal findings on chest auscultation does not rule out the possibility of cardiac pathology. \n3. The difficult-to-interpret ECG may be due to ongoing myocardial damage or changes in the ECG pattern that are difficult to distinguish from baseline abnormalities.\n4. Laboratory tests typically used to diagnose AMI include cardiac biomarkers such as creatine kinase (CK), CK-MB, troponin T, and troponin I. These biomarkers are released into the bloodstream when heart muscle cells are damaged or die.\n5. Of the listed laboratory tests, troponin T would have the most diagnostic utility in this patient. Troponin T is a highly sensitive and specific biomarker for myocardial injury, and elevated levels are indicative of ongoing or recent myocardial damage. CK-MB and lactate dehydrogenase are less specific and less sensitive than troponin T, and alanine aminotransferase and alkaline phosphatase are not cardiac-specific biomarkers.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c3016bef-96af-49aa-a5dc-9b787be2e4b2",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) Troponin T.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/30 04:08:01",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
