                    {
                        "id": "1bbbfc19-69e9-4127-84e0-0a55061c2149",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "zhou-01-ins",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the patient's condition and symptoms\nThe patient is a 59-year-old man who was hospitalized for acute myocardial infarction (heart attack) 5 days ago. He was treated with alteplase and has been under observation. He now complains of chest pain and discomfort in his left shoulder, similar to his initial symptoms.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the vital signs and physical examination findings\nThe patient's pulse, respiratory rate, and blood pressure are within normal limits. Auscultation of the chest reveals no abnormal findings.\n\nStep 3: Determine the most appropriate laboratory test for diagnosing the current issue\nThe patient's ECG is difficult to interpret, so we need to rely on laboratory tests to determine the cause of his chest pain. The most useful tests for diagnosing myocardial infarction are those that measure cardiac enzymes and proteins released into the bloodstream when heart muscle is damaged.\n\nA) Alanine aminotransferase (ALT) - This enzyme is primarily found in the liver and is not specific to heart muscle damage.\nB) Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) - This enzyme is found in various tissues, including the liver and bones, and is not specific to heart muscle damage.\nC) Creatine kinase (CK-MB) - This is a cardiac-specific isoenzyme of creatine kinase, which is released into the bloodstream when heart muscle is damaged. It is a useful marker for myocardial infarction.\nD) Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) - This enzyme is found in many tissues, including the heart, but is not specific to heart muscle damage.\nE) Troponin T - This is a cardiac-specific protein that is released into the bloodstream when heart muscle is damaged. It is a highly sensitive and specific marker for myocardial infarction.\n\nStep 4: Choose the most appropriate laboratory test for this patient\nGiven the patient's history and symptoms, the most useful laboratory test for diagnosing the cause of his chest pain would be one that is specific to heart muscle damage. Troponin T (E) is the most sensitive and specific marker for myocardial infarction, making it the best choice in this case.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "03b5519e-9d95-41d9-b9f1-2086adc8f521",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "E) Troponin T.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "E",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
