                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is presenting with chest pain, which is concerning for a possible recurrent myocardial infarction. The fact that he had an ST-elevation myocardial infarction 5 days ago also increases the suspicion for a recurrent event. \n\nThe vital signs are stable, but the patient's ECG is difficult to interpret, which suggests that there may be some abnormalities present. \n\nOut of the laboratory tests listed, the most useful one for diagnosing a recurrent myocardial infarction would be troponin T. Troponin T is a cardiac-specific biomarker that is released into the bloodstream when there is damage to the heart muscle. Elevated levels of troponin T would suggest that there is ongoing myocardial damage, which would support the diagnosis of a recurrent myocardial infarction. \n\nThe other laboratory tests listed (alanine aminotransferase, alkaline phosphatase, creatine kinase (CK-MB), and lactate dehydrogenase) are not specific to the heart and would not be as useful in diagnosing a recurrent myocardial infarction.",
