                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is presenting with chest pain and left shoulder discomfort, similar to his initial symptoms of acute myocardial infarction (AMI). Since he has already been diagnosed with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction and treated with alteplase, we should focus on laboratory tests that would help us determine if there is ongoing myocardial damage or a possible re-infarction.\n\nA) Alanine aminotransferase (ALT) - This enzyme is primarily found in the liver and is used to assess liver function. It would not be helpful in diagnosing ongoing myocardial damage.\n\nB) Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) - This enzyme is found in various tissues, including the liver and bones. It is not specific for myocardial damage and would not be helpful in this case.\n\nC) Creatine kinase (CK-MB) - This is an isoenzyme of creatine kinase that is specific to myocardial tissue. An elevated CK-MB level would indicate ongoing myocardial damage or a possible re-infarction. This would be a useful test in this case.\n\nD) Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) - This enzyme is found in many tissues, including the heart, liver, and muscles. While it can be elevated in myocardial infarction, it is not specific and would not be as helpful as CK-MB or troponin in this case.\n\nE) Troponin T - This is a cardiac-specific protein that is released into the bloodstream when there is damage to the heart muscle. An elevated troponin T level would indicate ongoing myocardial damage or a possible re-infarction. This would also be a useful test in this case.\n\nBetween CK-MB and Troponin T, troponin T is considered the more sensitive and specific marker for myocardial damage. Therefore, the correct answer is E) Troponin T.",
