                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is a 25-year-old woman who has had two uncomplicated vaginal deliveries at term. She has missed her menstrual cycle for 4 weeks and has a positive urine pregnancy test. She desires an elective abortion and wants to know the most appropriate procedure.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the different types of elective abortion procedures?\n2. What are the risks and benefits of each procedure?\n3. Which procedure is most appropriate for a patient with a positive urine pregnancy test and desire for elective abortion?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1:\n1. The different types of elective abortion procedures include:\n- Dilatation and evacuation (D&E): This involves dilating the cervix and removing the contents of the uterus using suction and instruments.\n- Oxytocin-induced labor: This involves using medication to induce labor and deliver the fetus vaginally.\n- Manual uterine aspiration: This involves using a hand-held syringe to remove the contents of the uterus.\n- Dilatation and curettage (D&C) after vacuum evacuation: This involves dilating the cervix and using a vacuum to remove the contents of the uterus.\n- Oral mifepristone + misoprostol: This involves taking medication to induce abortion.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\n2. The risks and benefits of each procedure are:\n- D&E: Benefits include a shorter procedure time and lower risk of complications compared to other surgical methods. Risks include bleeding, infection, and uterine perforation.\n- Oxytocin-induced labor: Benefits include a more natural delivery process and lower risk of complications compared to surgical methods. Risks include bleeding, infection, and the possibility of incomplete abortion.\n- Manual uterine aspiration: Benefits include a shorter procedure time and lower risk of complications compared to other surgical methods. Risks include bleeding, infection, and the possibility of incomplete abortion.\n- D&C after vacuum evacuation: Benefits include a shorter procedure time and lower risk of complications compared to other surgical methods. Risks include bleeding, infection, and the possibility of incomplete abortion.\n- Oral mifepristone + misoprostol: Benefits include a non-invasive method and the ability to have the abortion at home. Risks include bleeding, infection, and the possibility of incomplete abortion.\n\nAnswering sub-question 3:\n3. Based on the patient's age, previous vaginal deliveries, and desire for elective abortion, the most appropriate procedure would be Dilatation and evacuation (D&E). This procedure has a shorter",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "956a3e53-fbd8-432f-8de7-cb3e6766f23f",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Dilatation and evacuation (D&E).",
                                "answer_from_choices": "A",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
