                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the patient's gestational age, the safety and effectiveness of each procedure, and any contraindications or risks associated with each option.\n\nThe patient has missed her menstrual cycle for 4 weeks, which means she is likely around 4-5 weeks pregnant. Let's evaluate each option for elective termination of pregnancy in this patient:\n\nA) Dilatation and evacuation (D&E): This procedure is typically performed in the second trimester (13-24 weeks) of pregnancy. Since the patient is only 4-5 weeks pregnant, this option is not appropriate.\n\nB) Oxytocin-induced labor: This method is used for late second-trimester or third-trimester abortions. It is not suitable for early pregnancy termination.\n\nC) Manual uterine aspiration: This procedure is used for early pregnancy termination, typically up to 10 weeks gestation. It involves the use of a manual syringe to aspirate the contents of the uterus. This could be an appropriate option for the patient.\n\nD) Dilatation and curettage (D & C) after vacuum evacuation: This procedure is used for early pregnancy termination, typically up to 12-14 weeks gestation. It involves dilating the cervix and using a vacuum device to remove the contents of the uterus, followed by curettage to ensure complete removal. This could also be an appropriate option for the patient.\n\nE) Oral mifepristone + misoprostol: This medical abortion option is used for early pregnancy termination, typically up to 10 weeks gestation. It involves taking oral medications to induce a miscarriage. This is also an appropriate option for the patient.\n\nConsidering the patient's gestational age and the safety and effectiveness of each procedure, the most appropriate option for elective termination of pregnancy in this patient is:\n\nE) Oral mifepristone + misoprostol\n\nThis option is non-invasive, effective, and appropriate for the patient's gestational age. It is important for the patient to discuss this option with her healthcare provider to ensure she understands the process, potential side effects, and follow-up care.",
