                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is at 42 weeks' gestation, which is considered post-term. Her previous pregnancies were uncomplicated vaginal deliveries at 41 and 42 weeks' gestation. She has a BMI of 33 kg/m2.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the risks associated with post-term pregnancy?\n2. What is polyhydramnios?\n3. What is acute respiratory distress syndrome?\n4. What is caudal regression syndrome?\n5. What is meconium aspiration?\n6. What is placental abruption?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Risks associated with post-term pregnancy include fetal macrosomia, meconium aspiration, placental insufficiency, and stillbirth.\n2. Polyhydramnios is excessive amniotic fluid surrounding the fetus, which can increase the risk of preterm labor, premature rupture of membranes, and placental abruption.\n3. Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is a severe lung condition that can occur in newborns who have difficulty breathing. It is often caused by meconium aspiration or infection.\n4. Caudal regression syndrome is a rare congenital disorder that affects the development of the lower spine and legs. It can cause paralysis and other complications.\n5. Meconium aspiration occurs when a newborn inhales meconium (the baby's first stool) into their lungs, which can cause respiratory distress and other complications.\n6. Placental abruption is a serious condition in which the placenta separates from the uterine wall before delivery, which can cause heavy bleeding and fetal distress.\n\nAnswer: D) Meconium aspiration. The patient's child is at greatest risk for meconium aspiration, which can occur in post-term pregnancies and can be caused by fetal distress. The other complications listed are also risks associated with post-term pregnancy, but meconium aspiration is the most likely complication based on the patient's history and current presentation.",
