                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the risk factors and clinical presentation of the patient and relate them to the possible complications listed. \n\nThe patient is a 36-year-old woman, gravida 3, para 2, at 42 weeks' gestation. She has had two previous uncomplicated vaginal deliveries at 41 and 42 weeks' gestation. Her BMI is 33 kg/m2, which indicates obesity. The pregnancy has been uncomplicated, and her vital signs are within normal limits. The uterus is nontender and soft, consistent with a 42-weeks' gestation.\n\nNow, let's analyze each of the complications listed:\n\nA) Polyhydramnios: This is a condition characterized by an excessive amount of amniotic fluid. There is no information in the patient's history or presentation to suggest this complication.\n\nB) Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS): This is a life-threatening lung condition that can occur in critically ill patients. There is no indication that the patient or her baby is critically ill or at risk for ARDS.\n\nC) Caudal regression syndrome: This is a rare birth defect characterized by abnormal development of the lower spine. There is no information in the patient's history or presentation to suggest this complication.\n\nD) Meconium aspiration: This occurs when a newborn inhales a mixture of meconium (the baby's first stool) and amniotic fluid during labor or delivery. Risk factors for meconium aspiration include post-term pregnancy (beyond 40 weeks), maternal hypertension, and maternal smoking. In this case, the patient is at 42 weeks' gestation, which increases the risk of meconium aspiration.\n\nE) Placental abruption: This is a serious pregnancy complication in which the placenta detaches from the uterus before the baby is born. Risk factors include maternal hypertension, smoking, and previous placental abruption. There is no information in the patient's history or presentation to suggest this complication.\n\nBased on the patient's history and presentation, the child is at greatest risk for:\n\nD) Meconium aspiration",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "4763bcd3-5f5d-4c87-88bd-bf83f0c06b9d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Meconium aspiration.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
