                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient's symptoms suggest that she may have ingested a toxic plant that contains anticholinergic compounds. Anticholinergic toxicity can cause symptoms such as dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, and altered mental status. Treatment for anticholinergic toxicity typically involves administration of an anticholinesterase agent, which increases the availability of acetylcholine in the body and counteracts the effects of the toxic plant.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the symptoms of anticholinergic toxicity?\n2. What is the mechanism of action of anticholinesterase agents?\n3. Which of the listed drugs is an anticholinesterase agent?\n4. Which of the listed drugs is most appropriate for treating anticholinergic toxicity?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The symptoms of anticholinergic toxicity include dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, constipation, tachycardia, hyperthermia, altered mental status, and hallucinations.\n2. Anticholinesterase agents inhibit the breakdown of acetylcholine, which increases the availability of acetylcholine in the body and counteracts the effects of anticholinergic toxicity.\n3. The listed drugs that are anticholinesterase agents are physostigmine, pralidoxime, pyridostigmine, and neostigmine.\n4. Of the listed drugs, physostigmine is the most appropriate for treating anticholinergic toxicity. Atropine is not an anticholinesterase agent and would not be effective in treating anticholinergic toxicity. Pralidoxime is used to treat organophosphate poisoning, not anticholinergic toxicity. Pyridostigmine and neostigmine are used to treat myasthenia gravis and would not be effective in treating anticholinergic toxicity.",
