                    {
                        "id": "ba0d76aa-bbe8-4697-843d-152afba23f87",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: Which drug should be administered to a 35-year-old woman who presents with muscle spasms, blurred vision, dry mouth, hallucinations, dry red skin, enlarged pupils, and urinary retention after foraging plants in the woods?\n\nStep 1: Identify the cause of the symptoms\nThe patient's symptoms suggest that she may have ingested a plant containing anticholinergic toxins, such as those found in plants like deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna) or jimsonweed (Datura stramonium). These toxins can cause symptoms like muscle spasms, blurred vision, dry mouth, hallucinations, dry red skin, enlarged pupils, and urinary retention.\n\nStep 2: Determine the appropriate treatment\nThe appropriate treatment for anticholinergic poisoning is an antidote that can reverse the effects of the toxins. In this case, the antidote is a cholinesterase inhibitor, which can help restore the balance of acetylcholine in the body and alleviate the patient's symptoms.\n\nStep 3: Choose the correct drug from the options provided\nA) Atropine - This is an anticholinergic drug and would not be helpful in this situation.\nB) Physostigmine - This is a cholinesterase inhibitor and can be used to treat anticholinergic poisoning.\nC) Pralidoxime - This is used to treat organophosphate poisoning, not anticholinergic poisoning.\nD) Pyridostigmine - This is a cholinesterase inhibitor, but it is primarily used to treat myasthenia gravis and is not the best choice for anticholinergic poisoning.\nE) Neostigmine - This is also a cholinesterase inhibitor, but it is primarily used to treat myasthenia gravis and is not the best choice for anticholinergic poisoning.\n\nAnswer: B) Physostigmine is the most likely drug to be administered to this patient to treat her symptoms.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c1b32a4f-d65a-4c96-8bdf-b4bd2f1b87bf",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Physostigmine, as it is the most appropriate cholinesterase inhibitor to treat anticholinergic poisoning in this patient's situation.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
