                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 57-year-old woman with a 3-month history of fatigue, generalized itchiness, and yellowing of the eyes. She has a palpable liver edge and laboratory studies show elevated bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, AST, and ALT levels. Ultrasonography shows mildly increased echogenicity of the liver. The additional finding most likely to be found in this patient is:\n\nB) Cutaneous xanthomas\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the significance of the patient's symptoms of fatigue, itchiness, and yellowing of the eyes?\n- These symptoms suggest liver dysfunction or disease, as evidenced by the elevated bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, AST, and ALT levels.\n2. What is the significance of the palpable liver edge and increased echogenicity of the liver on ultrasonography?\n- These findings suggest liver enlargement and/or damage, which is consistent with the elevated liver function tests.\n3. What are cutaneous xanthomas?\n- Cutaneous xanthomas are yellowish, cholesterol-filled deposits that can appear on the skin in patients with hyperlipidemia or other lipid metabolism disorders.\n4. Why are cutaneous xanthomas most likely to be found in this patient?\n- The patient's elevated liver function tests suggest liver dysfunction, which can lead to impaired lipid metabolism and hyperlipidemia. This can result in the development of cutaneous xanthomas.",
