                        "cot": "Student: I'm not sure which option is the most appropriate next step in management. Can you help me work through this?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through each option and discuss its relevance to the situation. First, option A suggests advising the patient to stop masturbating. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: Well, I think masturbation is a normal part of adolescent development, so I don't think it's necessary to advise him to stop.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Masturbation is a normal part of sexual development, and there is no medical reason for him to stop. Now, let's look at option B, which suggests measuring serum testosterone levels. What are your thoughts on this?\n\nStudent: I don't see any reason to suspect that his testosterone levels are abnormal. He's the captain of his wrestling team and doing well in school, so it doesn't seem like there's a hormonal issue.\n\nProfessor: You're right. There's no indication that his testosterone levels are abnormal, so there's no need to measure them. Moving on to option C, which suggests referring the patient for behavioral therapy. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: I don't think he needs behavioral therapy because, as we discussed earlier, masturbation is a normal part of adolescent development.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. There's no indication that his behavior is problematic or requires therapy. Now, let's consider option D, which suggests reassuring the mother. What are your thoughts on this?\n\nStudent: I think this might be the best option because the mother seems to be the one who is concerned, and it would be helpful to reassure her that her son's behavior is normal.\n\nProfessor: I agree. The mother's concern seems to be the main issue here, and reassuring her that her son's behavior is normal and not a cause for concern would be the most appropriate next step. Finally, let's look at option E, which suggests prescribing sertraline. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: I don't think there's any reason to prescribe medication in this situation. There's no indication that the patient is experiencing any mental health issues.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. There's no indication that medication is necessary in this situation. So, based on our discussion, which option do you think is the most appropriate next step in management?\n\nStudent: I think the most appropriate next step is to reassure the mother, option D.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Good job working through the options and arriving at the right answer.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0575f1af-93e5-4648-9db8-9a790f3e0e8d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
