                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Assess the situation and gather information. The patient has lost significant amounts of blood and needs a blood transfusion to save his life. However, both the patient's wife and his medical chart indicate that he should not receive blood transfusions for religious reasons.\n\nStep 2: Consider the ethical principles involved. The principle of autonomy suggests that the patient's wishes should be respected, while the principle of beneficence suggests that the healthcare provider should act in the best interest of the patient, which in this case would be to administer a blood transfusion.\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the options. Administering a blood transfusion without consent (option A) would violate the patient's autonomy and could have legal consequences. Refusing to treat the patient (option E) would not be in the best interest of the patient and could also have legal consequences. Trying to convince the wife (option D) may not be effective and could further delay treatment.\n\nStep 4: Seek guidance from the appropriate authority. In this case, the ethics board of the hospital (option B) would be the appropriate authority to consult, as they can provide guidance on how to proceed in a way that respects the patient's wishes while also considering the best interest of the patient.\n\nStep 5: Follow the guidance provided by the ethics board and proceed with the treatment plan that they recommend.\n\nAnswer: B) Call the ethics board of the hospital to discuss the issue.",
                        "answers": [
