                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble deciding what the best course of action is in this scenario. Can you help me work through it?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through each option and discuss the implications. First, option A is to administer a blood transfusion to save the patient's life. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: Well, it would save his life, but it goes against his religious beliefs and his written instructions.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Now, let's look at option B, which is to call the ethics board of the hospital to discuss the issue. What are your thoughts on this?\n\nStudent: It seems like a good idea to get input from the ethics board since this is a difficult decision, but I'm not sure if there's enough time to do that since the patient needs emergency surgery.\n\nProfessor: Good point. Time is of the essence in this situation. Moving on to option C, which is to respect the patient's written instructions and not administer a blood transfusion. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: It respects the patient's wishes, but it could result in his death.\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's the dilemma we're facing. Now, let's consider option D, which is to convince the wife to administer a blood transfusion to her husband. What are your thoughts on this?\n\nStudent: It might be difficult to convince her since she also seems to be against blood transfusions for religious reasons. But if she agrees, it could save his life.\n\nProfessor: True, but it's important to remember that the patient's written instructions should be respected. Finally, let's discuss option E, which is to refuse to treat the patient as he will die without a blood transfusion. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: It doesn't seem ethical to refuse treatment, but I understand that the doctor might not want to perform the surgery if they believe the patient will die without a blood transfusion.\n\nProfessor: You're right, it's not ethical to refuse treatment. So, considering all the options and their implications, which one do you think is the best course of action?\n\nStudent: I think option B, calling the ethics board of the hospital to discuss the issue, might be the best choice. Even though time is limited, it's important to get input from the ethics board to make the most informed decision possible.\n\nProfessor: I agree. In this difficult situation, it's crucial to consult with the ethics board to ensure that the decision made is",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "9ebe48fc-0f63-4593-93de-1fe4278abb0d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
