                        "cot": "\nA) Administer a blood transfusion to save the patient's life\n\nThis is the only option that will save the patient's life. The patient has lost a significant amount of blood and needs a blood transfusion to survive. If you do not give him a blood transfusion, he will die.\n\nB) Call the ethics board of the hospital to discuss the issue\n\nThis is not a good option. The ethics board is not a medical board and will not be able to give you medical advice. They will also not be able to make a decision about what to do in this case.\n\nC) Respect the patient's written instructions and do not adminster a blood transfusion\n\nThis is not a good option. The patient's written instructions are to not receive a blood transfusion. However, he has also lost a significant amount of blood and needs a blood transfusion to survive. If you do not give him a blood transfusion, he will die.\n\nD) Convince the wife to administer a blood transfusion to her husband\n\nThis is not a good option. The wife has made it clear that under no circumstance should he receive a blood transfusion. If you try to convince her to change her mind, she may become angry and leave the hospital. If you do not give him a blood transfusion, he will die.\n\nE) Refuse to treat the patient as he will die without a blood transfusion",
