                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "zhou-01-ins",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms, let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Can form an endospore: This characteristic is associated with bacteria like Bacillus and Clostridium species. However, these bacteria typically cause food poisoning or wound infections, not the respiratory symptoms seen in this patient.\n\nB) Has a rabbit reservoir: This characteristic is associated with the bacterium Francisella tularensis, which causes tularemia. However, this patient's symptoms and history do not strongly suggest tularemia.\n\nC) Has no cell wall: This characteristic is associated with Mycoplasma species, which can cause atypical pneumonia. The patient's symptoms, including fever, cough, headache, and patchy infiltrates on chest radiograph, are consistent with atypical pneumonia.\n\nD) Is more common in parrot owners: This characteristic is associated with Chlamydia psittaci, which causes psittacosis. While this patient has some respiratory symptoms, there is no mention of exposure to birds, making this less likely.\n\nE) Is transmitted by air conditioning: This characteristic is associated with Legionella pneumophila, which causes Legionnaires' disease. However, this patient's history and symptoms do not strongly suggest Legionnaires' disease.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and the characteristics of the organisms, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is associated with the characteristic:\n\nC) Has no cell wall (Mycoplasma species causing atypical pneumonia)",
                        "answers": [
