                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "B) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error.\n\nCritique: This answer correctly defines a type 1 error, which is also known as a false positive or alpha (\u03b1) error. If the authors had encountered a type 1 error, it would mean that they incorrectly concluded that mepolizumab significantly reduced asthma exacerbations and improved markers of asthma control when, in reality, there was no true difference between the treatment and placebo groups.\n\nReconsidering other answer options:\nA) A type 1 error is not related to the study's power, which is the ability to detect a true difference between study groups. This statement describes a type 2 error, also known as a beta (\u03b2) error.\nC) This statement describes a type 2 error, not a type 1 error.\nD) This statement incorrectly defines a type 1 error as a beta (\u03b2) error. A type 1 error is an alpha (\u03b1) error, and its probability is usually set at 0.05 or 0.01.\nE) A type 1 error is related to the significance level (alpha) of a study, not the confidence interval.\n\nFinal answer: B) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error.",
                        "answers": [
