                        "cot": "\n\nD) Hydrochlorthiazide\n\nHydrochlorthiazide is a diuretic medication commonly used to treat hypertension. However, it can cause fluid and electrolyte imbalances, including lower extremity swelling, especially when used in combination with other anti-hypertensive medications. The patient's new onset lower extremity swelling is likely a side effect of the added medication. Metoprolol and verapamil are beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers, respectively, which can also be used to treat hypertension but are less likely to cause lower extremity swelling. Nifedipine is a calcium channel blocker that can cause peripheral edema, but it is less commonly used as a first-line anti-hypertensive medication. Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic that can be used to treat hypertension and edema, but it is not typically added as a second medication for hypertension control.",
