                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStep 1: Review the patient's history and current symptoms. The patient has a history of diabetes and poorly controlled hypertension, and is now complaining of new onset lower extremity swelling.\n\nStep 2: Consider the patient's current medications. The patient is currently taking enalapril and metformin.\n\nStep 3: Consider the medications that are commonly used to treat hypertension. Commonly used medications for hypertension include metoprolol, verapamil, nifedipine, hydrochlorothiazide, and spironolactone.\n\nStep 4: Consider the side effects of each medication. Metoprolol, verapamil, and nifedipine are all beta blockers and calcium channel blockers, respectively, and are not known to cause lower extremity swelling. Hydrochlorothiazide is a diuretic and can cause lower extremity swelling as a side effect. Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic and can also cause lower extremity swelling as a side effect.\n\nAnswer: D) Hydrochlorothiazide",
                        "answers": [
