                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nFirst, let's rephrase the question to make sure we understand it correctly: The patient's primary care physician added another anti-hypertensive medication to the patient's regimen one month ago, and now the patient is complaining of new onset lower extremity swelling. Which of the following medications was likely prescribed to this patient?\n\nSecond, let's work this out step by step by spelling out our thoughts & reasoning so anyone can verify them:\n\nThe patient has poorly controlled hypertension, so the physician likely prescribed a medication to help lower the patient's blood pressure. Of the five medications listed, Metoprolol, Verapamil, Nifedipine, Hydrochlorthiazide, and Spironolactone, the three that are commonly used to treat hypertension are Metoprolol, Verapamil, and Nifedipine. Since the patient is also taking enalapril, which is an ACE inhibitor, the physician likely prescribed one of the other two medications, either Verapamil or Nifedipine.\n\nThird, make sure we have the right answer: The likely medication prescribed to this patient is either Verapamil (B) or Nifedipine (C).",
