                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the microscopic finding observed in a patient with flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis are characteristic clinical features of lichen planus.\n- Lichen planus is a dermatological condition that affects the skin, mucous membranes, and nails.\n- Microscopic examination of skin lesions in lichen planus typically shows a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis, along with various epidermal changes.\n- The epidermal changes in lichen planus can include hyperkeratosis, hypergranulosis, and acanthosis, as well as basal cell degeneration, suprabasal split, and acantholysis.\n- Basal cell degeneration refers to the separation of the basal layer of the epidermis from the underlying dermis, which can be seen in some cases of lichen planus.\n- Suprabasal split refers to the separation of the epidermis above the basal layer, which can also be seen in some cases of lichen planus.\n- Acantholysis refers to the loss of intercellular connections between keratinocytes, resulting in the formation of intraepidermal clefts or vesicles. This is a hallmark feature of pemphigus vulgaris, but can also be seen in some cases of lichen planus.\n\nAnswer: D) Acantholysis",
                        "answers": [
