                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the correct answer, let's first analyze the symptoms presented by the patient:\n\n1. Flat topped violaceous papules: These are flat, purple-colored skin lesions that can be indicative of various skin conditions, including lichen planus.\n2. Thinning of nails: This can be a sign of nail dystrophy, which is also associated with lichen planus.\n3. Onycholysis: This refers to the separation of the nail from the nail bed, which can also be a symptom of lichen planus.\n\nBased on these symptoms, it is likely that the patient is suffering from lichen planus. Now, let's analyze the microscopic findings associated with lichen planus to determine the correct answer:\n\nA) Prominent necrotic cell: Necrotic cells are dead cells, and while they can be present in various skin conditions, they are not specifically associated with lichen planus.\n\nB) Suprabasal split: This refers to a split in the skin above the basal layer. This finding is more commonly associated with pemphigus vulgaris, not lichen planus.\n\nC) Basal cell degeneration: This is a characteristic microscopic finding in lichen planus. In this condition, the basal layer of the epidermis undergoes degeneration, leading to the formation of the characteristic skin lesions.\n\nD) Acantholysis: This refers to the loss of cell-to-cell adhesion within the epidermis, leading to the separation of individual cells. This finding is more commonly associated with pemphigus vulgaris, not lichen planus.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and the microscopic findings associated with lichen planus, the correct answer is:\n\nC) Basal cell degeneration",
