                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis. These symptoms are characteristic of lichen planus, an inflammatory skin condition. Lichen planus affects the skin, nails, and mucous membranes and is characterized by the presence of pruritic, polygonal, flat-topped, violaceous papules.\n\nTo determine the microscopic finding observed in this patient, we need to know the characteristic histopathological features of lichen planus.\n\nSub-question 1: What are the histopathological features of lichen planus?\n\nAnswer: The histopathological features of lichen planus include:\n1. Hyperkeratosis and thickening of the granular layer (orthokeratosis)\n2. Saw-tooth appearance of the rete ridges\n3. Liquefactive degeneration of the basal cell layer\n4. Band-like lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermoepidermal junction\n5. Civatte bodies (necrotic keratinocytes) in the basal layer\n\nNow, let's match these features with the given options:\n\nA) Prominent necrotic cell: This corresponds to Civatte bodies, which are seen in lichen planus.\nB) Suprabasal split: This is a feature of pemphigus vulgaris, not lichen planus.\nC) Basal cell degeneration: This corresponds to liquefactive degeneration of the basal cell layer, which is seen in lichen planus.\nD) Acantholysis: This is a feature of pemphigus vulgaris, not lichen planus.\n\nBased on the histopathological features of lichen planus, the microscopic finding observed in this patient is C) Basal cell degeneration.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "68cfa396-dab6-4d7f-8201-534791f7e1a0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
