                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I am having trouble understanding this question about a patient with flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis. Can you help me work through it step by step?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by identifying the condition that the patient is presenting with. Do you know what condition is associated with these symptoms?\n\nStudent: I think it might be lichen planus.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Now, let's look at the answer choices and see which one is associated with lichen planus. Do you know what the microscopic finding is in lichen planus?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. Can we go through each option?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Option A is \"prominent necrotic cell.\" Necrotic cells are dead cells, and while they can be present in many conditions, they are not the primary microscopic finding in lichen planus. So, we can eliminate option A.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. What about option B, \"suprabasal split\"?\n\nProfessor: A suprabasal split refers to a separation between the layers of the epidermis above the basal layer. This is a characteristic finding in pemphigus vulgaris, not lichen planus. So, we can eliminate option B as well.\n\nStudent: Alright. Now, we have option C, \"basal cell degeneration,\" and option D, \"acantholysis.\" Can you explain these terms?\n\nProfessor: Certainly. Basal cell degeneration refers to the damage or degeneration of the basal cells in the epidermis. Acantholysis, on the other hand, refers to the loss of connections between keratinocytes, which are the cells that make up most of the epidermis.\n\nStudent: So, which one of these is associated with lichen planus?\n\nProfessor: In lichen planus, the primary microscopic finding is basal cell degeneration. This is due to an immune response targeting the basal cells, leading to their damage and degeneration. So, the correct answer is option C.\n\nStudent: I see. So, the correct answer is C) Basal cell degeneration because that is the primary microscopic finding in lichen planus. Thank you for helping me work through this question, Professor!\n\nProfessor: You're welcome! I'm glad I could help you understand the reasoning behind the answer. If you have any more questions, don't hesitate to ask.",
